    ('06', '01', '939', '176',
     'Architects (Architectural Practice)', 'Fees: Application for Eligibility Evaluation: $100; National Council of Architectural Registration Boards (NCARB) Architect Registration Examination (ARE): $235 per division - six divisions; California Supplemental Exam (CSE): $100;  License: $400 every two years.
Examination Frequency:  ARE: by computer, Monday-Saturday at most locations;
CSE: by computer, Monday-Saturday at most locations.
Exam Locations: ARE: numerous locations throughout California, as well as locations across the U.S., Guam, Puerto Rico, and in Canada; CSE: nearly 40 locations throughout California and across the U.S.
Experience Requirements: ARE: five net years of experience through: education or training (work) experience, or a combination of each. Work experience must be under the direct supervision of an architect;
CSE: the preceding experience requirements, completion of the NCARB ARE, and completion of the NCARB Architectural Experience Program.
Licensure: successful completion of the CSE and its preceding requirements, and submission of fingerprints for a criminal background clearance.
Average Time to Process Application: 30 days or less for Application for Eligibility Evaluation or California Architect Reciprocity Application. Approximately 30 days or less for Application for Licensure – dependent upon receipt of DOJ and FBI clearances to issue a license.
Renewal Period: Biennial (odd years) on the last day of an individual’s birth month.
Special License Requirements: 
To renew a license, architects must complete five (5) hours of continuing education (CE) on disability access requirements and an additional five (5) hours CE on Zero Net Carbon Design for a total of 10 CE hours.
Compliance with U.S. and state tax requirements and family support.
Exceptions for license are listed in BPC section 5537 which summarized are: 1) single-family woodframe construction not more than two stories and basement in height; 2) multiple dwellings containing not more than four dwelling units of woodframe construction not more than two stories and basement in height. No more than four dwelling units per lot; 3) garages or other structures appurtenant to single-family dwelling, of woodframe construction not more than two stories and basement in height; 4) agricultural and ranch buildings of woodframe construction, etc.
Authority: Business and Professions Code (BPC), Division 3, Chapter 3, Sections 5500-5610.7.
California Code of Regulations, Title 16, Division 2, Sections 100-160.',
