lization requirements for Latin. (a) Plan One. A bachelor’s or higher degree with an undergraduate or graduate major in Latin; or (b) Plan Two. A bachelor’s or higher degree with thirty (30) semester hours in Latin to include credit in the areas specified below: 1. Latin vocabulary, grammar, and composition; 2. Latin literature; and, 3. Roman culture; or (c) Plan Three. A bachelor’s or higher degree with specialization requirements completed in one (1) of the modern languages as specified in paragraph (1)(a) or (b) of this rule, and twenty-one (21) semester hours in Latin to include credit in the areas specified below: 1. Latin vocabulary, grammar, and composition; 2. Latin literature; and, 3. Roman culture.',
