    ('15', '01', '0000000019', '010',
     'Electrologist', 'DESCRIPTION OF OCCUPATION:
	An Electrologist removes hair from skin using needles and electrical equipment.

REQUIREMENTS FOR LICENSING:
	Hawaii does not reciprocate with any jurisdiction, therefore, a person shall apply, qualify, be tested, and pay the necessary fees to become licensed in Hawaii.
	
	Personal requirements:  1) Be 18 years of age.   2) Be a U.S. citizen, a U.S. national, or an alien authorized to work in the U.S.

	Experience and education requirements:  Have electrology training (schooling/apprenticeship) in compliance with Hawaii''s curriculum:  a) Recognized schooling totaling 600 hours;  or b) Qualified apprenticeship totaling 800 hours; or  c) Combination schooling and apprenticeship totaling 700 hours.  Experience is recognized only after licensure and/or completion of training, and for the purpose of satisfying differences in the training requirements. Experience totaling 1 year is necessary, any experience totaling less than 1 year will not be recognized. Submit copies of all electrologist licenses.

	Disqualification for licensure:  1) Criminal conviction associated with the electrologist''s practice;  2) Declared mentally incompetent by a court; and  3) Administrative disciplinary action against the electrologist''s license.

EXAMINATIONS:
	Exam administered by a professional testing service.

FEES:
$50 for application, $96 for 2-year license, and $100 for the biennial compliance resolution fund. License renewal every 2 years is $96.

