    ('31', '01', '1130310000', 'BF1',
     'Bank Executive Officer', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>Bank Executive Officers direct and coordinate financial activities of workers in a main bank location or branch office. Duties are potentially wide-ranging, but may include making loans or investments, exercising management functions or major policy-making functions, or exercising substantial employee supervision duties.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>Good moral character; 
Known integrity; 
Business experience & responsibility;
Capable of conducting the affairs of a bank on sound financial principles.<br><h3>Fees</h3><br>License: $50.00<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>No Renewal Fee',
     '0', '9', '9', '9', '9',
     '9', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'ndbf.nebraska.gov', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1720510001', 'EA2',
     'Engineer, Professional', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>A Professional Engineer is a person who is licensed by the Board to practice engineering. 
Practice of engineering means any service or creative work that requires engineering education, training, and experience in the application of special knowledge of the mathematical, physical, and engineering sciences. The services may include, but not be limited to: Planning; Providing studies, designs, drawings, specifications, and other technical submissions; Administering construction contracts.
The practice of engineering does NOT include the practice of architecture.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>A person applying for licensure as a professional engineer in Nebraska must submit a completed applicationalong with applicable filing fees. A separate application is required for each discipline the licensee wishes to be licensed.  Initial Licensure Education- A degree from an ABET/EAC or CACB-accredited engineering program.  Persons not holding a degree from an ABET/EAC or CACB-accredited engineering program must have their education evaluated by National Council of Examiners for Engineering and Surveying (NCEES) Credentials Evaluations to determine if the individual’s education meets the NCEES Education Standard. Any deficiencies must be removed prior to approval by the Board. Examination- Successful passage the Fundamentals of Engineering (FE) Exam and the Principles and Practice of Engineering. All exams are administered through NCEES for the Board. For exam specifications, visit the NCEES website. Experience- Show satisfactory evidence of four years of progressive engineering experience acceptable to the Board. A master’s Degree in engineering may count toward one year experience, and a doctorate in engineering may count toward two years’ experience. Licensure by Comity Hold an NCEES Record in good standing, which verifies that the applicant meets NCEES Model Law Engineer (MLE) standards;-OR Meet the initial licensure requirements in Nebraska;-OR Have been licensed for a minimum of 15 years as a Professional Engineer in at least one other licensing jurisdiction in good standing.  All Professional Engineer license candidates must also pass an exam on the statutes, rules, andother requirements unique to Nebraska. Please see ea.nebraska.gov/engineer-initial-licensure for more information. <br><h3>Fees</h3><br>Fees are set by the Board and are subject to change. Check the Board’s website for current fees. 
License Application Filing Fee- $150.00 
PE Examination Application Filing Fee- $30.00 (Additional testing fees are required after approval by the Board, and are determined by the exam administrator) 
PE Examination Retake Application Filing Fee- $30 (Additional testing fees are required after approval by the Board, and are determined by the exam administrator) 
Fees related to the FE exam will be determined by NCEES.<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Renewal is due biennially by December 31. Individuals with last names starting with the letters A through K renew in odd-numbered years; individuals with last names starting with the letters L through Z renew in even-numbered years. 
The renewal fee is $80.00.
Late Renewal Fee- 10% of biennial fee each month late, but late fees may not exceed the amount of the regular biennial fee. 
After a license has been expired for one year or more, a new application is required and licensee must meet the current licensing requirements in place at the time of application.',
     '0', '0', '0', '0', '9',
     '0', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'ea.nebraska.gov', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1140110000', 'HH3',
     'Funeral Director & Embalmer', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>Funeral Directing- 
Funeral Directing is counseling families or next of kin in regards to the conduct of a funeral service for a dead human body for burial, disposition, or cremation, and/or directing or supervising burial, disposition, or cremation of dead human bodies.
Funeral Directors may also provide for or maintain a funeral establishment.
Only licensed Funeral Directors may represent themselves as or use in connection with their own name the title of funeral director, mortician, or any other title implying that he or she is engaged in the business of funeral directing.

Embalming- 
Embalming means preparing dead human bodies for burial or other final disposal by a licensed funeral director. 
Embalming may also include requesting and obtaining burial or removal permits, or assuming any of the other duties incident to the practice of embalming. 
Only licensed Funeral Directors & Embalmers or their apprentices may engage in:
1. The disinfection and preservation of dead human beings, in entirety or in part;
2. The attempted disinfection and preservation thereof by the use or application of chemical substances, fluids, or gases ordinarily used, prepared, or intended for such purposes, either by outward application of such chemical substances, fluids, or gases on the body, or introducing them into the body, by vascular or hypodermic injection, or by direct introduction into the organs or cavities.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>Applicants must:1. Present satisfactory proof that the applicant has earned the equivalent of 40 semester hours ofcollege credit, in addition to a full course of instruction in an accredited school of mortuary science.Such hours must include the equivalent of:A. Six semester hours of English;B. Twelve semester hours of BusinessC. Four semester hours of Chemistry;D. Twelve semester hours of a biological science relating to the human body;E. Six semester hours of psychology or counseling.2. Present proof to the department that he or she has completed the following training:A. A full course of instruction in an accredited school of mortuary science;B. A 12-month apprenticeship under the supervision of a licensed Funeral Director and Embalmerpracticing in Nebraska. This apprenticeship must consist of arterially embalming 25 bodies andassisting with 25 funerals;C. Successful completion of the licensure examination approved by the Board;3. Provide satisfactory evidence that he or she is of good character and has attained the age of 19;4. Provide proof that he or she is either a citizen of the United States, an alien lawfully admitted into the United States who is eligible for a credential under the Uniform Credentialing Act, or a nonimmigrant whose visa for entry, or application for visa for entry, is related to such employment in the United States.<br><h3>Fees</h3><br>Initial License- $90.00
Apprentice License- $25.00<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Renewal InformationRenewal is required biennially by February 1 of even-numbered years. The renewal fee is $90.00.During the 24 months prior to the license expiration date, each licensed funeral director, embalmer, or funeral director and embalmer must complete: (A) 12 hours of acceptable continuing education hours; and (B) Pass the Nebraska jurisprudence examination with a score of at least a 75%.',
     '0', '2', '2', '0', '9',
     '0', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'dhhs.ne.gov/licensure/pages/professions-and-occupations.aspx', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1190130000', 'RG1',
     'Race Horse Owner', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>A Race Horse Owner has an interest in the racehorse as an owner, part-owner, or lessee, or is the responsible managing owner of a corporation, syndicate, or partnership which is the legal owner of such a racehorse.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>Race Horse Owners must be at least 16 years old, and must be able to show ownership documents to properly identify any horse intended to be raced.  This license permits an Owner to work on the backside, caring only for their horse(s) at a Nebraska race track.<br><h3>Fees</h3><br>License Fee - $30.00<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Renewal is required annually by December 31.  The renewal fee is $30.00.Fingerprints are required every 5 years.',
     '0', '9', '9', '9', '9',
     '9', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'racingcommission.nebraska.gov', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1190210000', 'LB1',
     'Contractor/Sub-contractor', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>Contractors, general contractors and sub-contractors and any other person who arranges for the performance of or isengaged in the business of construction. Construction is work done on real property and annexations including newwork, additions, alterations, renovations, reconstruction, installations & repairs performed at one or more differentsites. All contractors and sub-contractors doing business in Nebraska must register. <br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>Applicants must complete an online registration application & where applicable, submit a certificate of Workers’Compensation Insurance.<br><h3>Fees</h3><br>25<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Annually from the date of  initial submitted application; $25.00',
     '0', '0', '0', '0', '2',
     '0', '0', '9', 'A', '0',
     'dol.nebraska.gov', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1190320000', 'ED1',
     'Education Administrator, Elementary & Secondary', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>Education Administrators plan, direct, and/or coordinate the academic, clerical, and/or auxiliary activities of public or private elementary or secondary schools.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>The Department of Education may issue standard, professional, and provisional administrative permits or certificates.Applicants must:1. Complete the appropriate application forms prescribed by the Department;2. Submit an official transcript of all college credit earned;3. Pay the prescribed fee;4. Not have an application for a certificate or permit denied or currently have a certificate or permit suspended or revoked by another jurisdiction, and not have any felony conviction, or any misdemeanor conviction involving abuse, neglect, or sexual misconduct;5. Qualify for or hold a Nebraska standard or professional teaching certificate, or hold a comparableand equivalent certificate;6. Submit evidence of human relations training;7. Submit evidence of basic skills competency (if applying for an entry-level Nebraska administrativecertificate after July 31, 1989);8. Submit evidence of special education training (if applying for the first issuance of a Nebraska administrative certificate after September 1, 1992);9. Submit a complete, legible set of the applicant''s fingerprints and pay a processing fee for a criminal history record check, if the applicant is seeking the first issuance of a Nebraska certificate and has not been a resident of Nebraska for at least five continuous years immediately preceding the date of application;10. Be of good moral character;11. Not have an emotional or mental incapacity to practice the profession;12. Submit evidence of content test passage, if applying for a new administrative endorsement.Standard Administrative Certificate-The Standard Administrative Certificate shall be valid for teaching and administration in all Nebraskaschool systems, except that such certificate shall be valid for the position of Superintendent of Schools only if so endorsed. If requested by the applicant at the time of application, such certificateshall be valid only in Nebraska nonpublic school systems.The Standard Administrative Certificate expires on August 31 of the fifth year on the certificate.Each applicant for a first Nebraska Standard Administrative Certificate must:1. Fulfill the requirements listed above;2. Have received a master''s degree in education or a subject area for which there is an endorsement from a standard institution of higher education with an approved program for the preparation of school administrators in the area(s) of endorsement sought by the applicant;3. Qualify for or hold one or more educational administration endorsements;4. Either:A. Within five years prior to the date of application, have received six semester hours of graduatecredit for course work;-OR-B. Within five years prior to the date of application, have administered half-time or more for oneyear in an accredited or approved school or educational service unit in another state wherein the applicant held a standard administrative certificate or its equivalent.Professional Administrative Certificate-The Professional Administrative Certificate shall be valid for teaching and administration in all Nebraska school systems, except that such certificate shall be valid for the position of Superintendent of Schools only if so endorsed. If so requested by the applicant at the time of application, such certificate shall be valid only in Nebraska nonpublic school systems.The Professional Administrative Certificate expires on August 31 of the tenth year on the certificate.Each applicant for a Professional Administrative Certificate must:1. Fulfill the requirements listed above;2. Have received a six-year specialist''s certificate or a doctorate degree in educational administrationfrom a standard institution of higher education with an approved program for the preparation of school administrators in the area(s) of endorsement sought by the applicant;3. Qualify for or hold one or more administrative endorsements;4. Either:A. Within five years prior to the date of application, have received six semester hours of graduatecredit for course work recognized as meeting all or part of the requirements for an administrativeendorsement;-OR-B. Within five years prior to the date of application, have administered half-time or more forone school year in the same accredited or approved school system or educational service unit,have been employed as a regular faculty member in the teacher or administrator preparationprogram of a standard institution of higher education, or in the Department while holding aNebraska standard administrative certificate or a comparable and equivalent certificate.Licensing requirements for this occupation are subject to change. For the most up-to-date requirements, please visit: https://www.education.ne.gov/tcert/administrative-certificates-and-permits/<br><h3>Fees</h3><br>Certificate/Renewal Fee: $75.00 for all schools; $55.00 for nonpublicAdditional Endorsement: $55.00 Fingerprinting and criminal history background checks are required for any person applying for his or her first Nebraska certificate, IF he or she has not lived in Nebraska for five continuous years. This requires an additional fee of $50.00, in addition to the regular application fee.<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Standard and Professional Administrative Certificate must be renewed prior to expiration or less thangive years after expiration.Each applicant for renewal of a Standard or Professional Administrative Certificate prior to orless than five years after the date of expiration of such certificate must:1. Fulfill the licensure requirements;2. Hold or have held a Nebraska standard or professional administrative certificate which shall nothave expired, or which expired less than five years prior to the date of application;3. Either:A. Within five years prior to the date of application, have administered half-time or more for oneschool year in the same Nebraska school system, a pre-kindergarten early childhood programwhich is required by law to employ teachers with certificates, an accredited or approved schoolsystem in another state, a U.S. Department of Defense school system, or have been employedas a regular faculty member in the teacher or administrator preparation program of a standardinstitution of higher education or been employed in the Department;-OR-B. Have received six semester hours of graduate credit from a standard institution of higher education within five years prior to the date of application.Licensing requirements for this occupation are subject to change. For the most up-to-date requirements, please visit: https://www.education.ne.gov/tcert/administrative-certificates-and-permits/',
     '0', '9', '9', '9', '9',
     '9', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'education.ne.gov/tcert/administrative-certificates-and-permits', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1190410000', 'TR1',
     'County Highway/City Street Superintendent', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>Licensed Superintendents work for local governing bodies at the county and/or municipal level. They assist in administering county and municipal road and street and programs; preparation of the one-year and six-year plans or programs, and road and street budgets; overseeing the annual program for road and street design, construction, and maintenance; and coordinating plans with adjacent government units.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>The sole requirement for the basic license (Class B) is successful completion of a six-hour written examination. 
A series of online training videos; five one-day pre-exam training classes; and a one-day exam preparation class is offered to applicants in preparation of the written examination. 
It is also desirable that applicants have work experience or education in road/street maintenance, design, construction, or administration.  
An advanced license (Class A) is open to those who have held a Class B License for two full calendar years and who have been employed as a superintendent for two years, or who have four years of comparable experience.<br><h3>Fees</h3><br>Examination & License (Class B)- $25.00
License (Class A)- $75.00
Examinations are administered in April and October. The application fee entitles an applicant to take the examination twice.<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Renewal is required every three years. Renewal fees are $30.00 for a Class B license and $50.00 for a Class A license.
There is no mandatory requirement for continuing education for the basic license. However, seminars are offered by the Department of Transportation and the University of Nebraska-Lincoln, and are attended on a voluntary basis.',
     '0', '9', '9', '9', '9',
     '9', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'dot.nebraska.gov/business-center/lpa/boards-liaison', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1191110000', 'HH7',
     'Nursing Home Administrator', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>Nursing Home Administrator- An individual who is responsible for the operation of a nursing home.
Provisional License- A license for a person otherwise not qualified for licensure as a Nursing Home Administrator who is selected to serve as the Administrator in order to maintain daily operations.
Nursing Home Administrator Responsible/Overseeing More Than 1 Facility, or the Dual Role of Administrator and Department Head- An Administrator may be responsible for and oversee the operation of up to three licensed facilities or may act in the dual role of Administrator and Department Head (but not in the dual role of Administrator and Director of Nursing).
Nursing Home Administrator of a Facility Caring Primarily for Persons with Head Injuries and Associated Disorders- An individual who will function as the Administrator of a facility caring primarily for persons with head injuries and associated disorders.
Mentoring/Administrator in Training (AIT)- A person who is training to become a Nursing Home Administrator and is supervised in a Nebraska nursing home by a Certified Preceptor. This requires completion of at least 640 hours of training and experience, which must be completed in not less than 4 calendar months, and must be at least 20 hours per week.
Certified Preceptor- A person who is currently licensed by the State of Nebraska as a Nursing Home Administrator and is approved by the department to supervise a person in a mentoring or AIT program. He or she must have three years of experience as a Nursing Home Administrator and have practiced within the last two years.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>Applicants must:1. Have passed both the NHA exam and CORE exam through the National Association of Long Term Care Administrator Boards (NAB) for Nursing Home Administrators;2. Meet one of the following education & experience requirements:A. Associate degree and:(a) If the applicant has at least two years of experience working full-time in a nursing home,or previous work experience in health care administration, he or she may complete either theMentoring Program or the Administrator-in-Training program;(b) Applicants who do not have at least two full years of experience working in a nursing home must complete the Administrator-in-Training program;B. Bachelor’s, master’s, or doctorate degree (in any subject) and completion of Mentoring Program and completion of Core Educational Requirements coursework. Core Educational Requirements means a minimum of three coursework hours in each of the following areas (to determine whether specific courses satisfy these requirements, contact DHHS):. Patient Care & Services;. Social Services;. Financial Management;. Administration;. Rules, Regulations, & Standards Relating to the Operation of a Health Care Facility;C. Bachelor''s, master''s, or doctorate degree in health care or health care administration and:(a) If the applicant has a bachelor''s or advanced degree in health care/health care administrationand two year''s of previous work experience in health care administration, he or she is not required to complete either the Mentoring Program or Administrator-in-Training program;(b) If the applicant has a bachelor’s or advanced degree in health care/health care administrationand does not have previous work experience in health care administration, he or she mustcomplete the Mentoring Program;D. Nursing degree:(a) If the applicant has a nursing degree and a full two years of previous work experience in health care administration, he or she is not required to complete either the Mentoring Program or the Administrator-in-Training program;(b) If the applicant has a nursing degree and does not have previous work experience in healthcare administration, he or she must have completed coursework related to the core educationalrequirements (see above) and complete the Mentoring Program;3. Be at least 19 years of age;4. If any disciplinary action was taken against the applicant’s license/registration by another state,applicant must submit a letter of explanation of the disciplinary action.<br><h3>Fees</h3><br>Nursing Home Administrator License Fee- $166.00, or $41.50 if initial license is obtained within six months prior to the next expiration date.
Continuing Education Program Fee- $25.00
Administrator-in-Training or Mentoring Program Fee- $50.00
Certified Preceptor License Fee- $25.00
Provisional Nursing Home Administrator License Fee- $110.00<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Nursing Home Administrator- Renewal is required biennially by December 31 of each even-numbered year. The renewal fee is $166.00.
At least 50 hours of approved continuing education must be completed during the prior 24 months to qualify for renewal. Licensees that complete more than 50 hours during a renewal period may carry a maximum of 25 hours earned into the next renewal period.
Provisional License- A provisional license can be issued for no more than 180 calendar days, and may not be renewed. The board may grant an extension not to exceed 90 days if the person seeking the provisional license is in a mentoring program.
Certified Preceptor- Renewal is required every four years, by December 31. The renewal fee is $25.00.
A Certified Preceptor must complete at least eight hours of approved preceptor training during the preceding 48 months to qualify for renewal. Preceptors who supervise a trainee in an Administrator-in-Training or Mentorship program will be granted eight hours towards this requirement (or 16 for supervising two trainees), which may also be counted towards renewal of the Preceptor’s underlying Nursing Home Administrator license.',
     '0', '2', '2', '0', '9',
     '2', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'dhhs.ne.gov/licensure/pages/professions-and-occupations.aspx', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1310110000', 'SS3',
     'Athlete Agent', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>Athlete Agent is defined as an individual who enters into an agency contract with a student-athlete or, directly or indirectly, recruits a student-athlete to enter into an agency contract <br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>They must complete the required forms provided by our office. <br><h3>Fees</h3><br>Initial License Fee - $300.00Reciprocal License Fee - $150.00<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Required to renew every 2 years. Renewal fee is $150.00',
     '0', '9', '9', '9', '9',
     '9', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'sos.nebraska.gov/licensing/athlete-agents', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1310110001', 'NA1',
     'Boxing & Martial Arts Support Occupations', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>Martial Arts means any of several arts of combat and self-defense that are recognized and practiced as a sport, including, but not limited to: 
• boxing;
• wrestling;
• kickboxing;
• karate;
• judo;
• taekwondo;
• aikido;
• jujutsu;
• krav maga;
• muay thai;
• kung fu.
Promoter- A person or entity licensed to organize and promote professional boxing or martial arts events. The term “promoter” includes any association, corporation, organization, club, partnership, or other entity that organizes and conducts boxing or MMA events, and any employee thereof.
Referee- A person licensed to ensure that a bout is conducted according to the appropriate rules.
Attending Physician- A person who conducts diagnostic blood tests, physical examinations, or other medical procedures for a boxing or MMA event. He or she must be a Doctor of Medicine or Doctor of Osteopathy licensed to practice in Nebraska.
Manager- A person who acts as a contestant’s representative to negotiate bouts and contracts.
Matchmaker- A person who matches contestants according to ability, weight, experience, etc. A licensed matchmaker is required for professional boxing events, and professional or amateur MMA events.
Judge- A person licensed to score a professional boxing or MMA round. Amateur event judges do not require a license.
Timekeeper- A person licensed to signal when a round is over and when ten seconds of a round remains.
Second- A person who is licensed and authorized to sit in a designated ringside area in order to coach and provide other assistance to a contestant, and who may to stop a bout if, in the opinion of the second, the contestant is unable to safely continue.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>All licenses must be approved by the Athletic Commissioner. To obtain a license, submit a form downloaded from the Commission website and pay the appropriate fee. 
All persons required to be licensed must be licensed prior to the start of an event in which they will participate. Promoters must be licensed at least 30 days prior to the event.
For specific requirements for each occupation, review Neb. Admin. Code 38 or consult the Nebraska Athletic Commission. <br><h3>Fees</h3><br>Professional Boxing or Wrestling Promoter- $200.00
Referee- $35.00
Attending Physician- $20.00
Manager- $20.00
Matchmaker- $50.00
Judge- $20.00
Timekeeper- $20.00
Second- $20.00<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>All licenses expire June 30 following the date of issuance.',
     '0', '9', '9', '9', '9',
     '9', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'athcomm.nebraska.gov', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1310210000', 'AG5',
     'Livestock Dealer', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>Livestock Dealers engage in the business of buying or selling livestock for the purpose of resale within the state.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>Applicants must complete an annual written application, and a $5,000.00 bond is required.<br><h3>Fees</h3><br>Application Fee- $50.00<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Renewal is required annually by October 1.',
     '0', '0', '0', '0', '9',
     '0', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'nda.nebraska.gov', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1310210001', 'AG3',
     'Nursery Stock Distributor', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>A Nursery Stock Distributor is any person who is involved in: 
The acquisition and further distribution of nursery stock; 
The utilization of nursery stock for landscaping or purchase of nursery stock for other persons;
The distribution of nursery stock with a mechanical digger, commonly known as a tree spade, or by other means; 
The solicitation of or taking orders for sales of nursery stock in the state; 
The growing and distribution of nursery stock or active involvement in the management or supervision of a nursery.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br> <br><h3>Fees</h3><br>The license fee is based on the amount of acreage on which nursery stock is held or grown. The fee is $83.00 for any amount of land up to one acre, and $3.61 for each additional acre. Licensure applications must be received prior to the beginning of distribution. If not received priorto the beginning of distribution, initial applications shall be considered delinquent and shall have anadditional late fee assessed of 25% per month.<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>A Nursery Stock Distributor license is valid from January 1 through December 31. 
Renewals are due on January 1 of each year.',
     '0', '9', '9', '9', '9',
     '9', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'nda.nebraska.gov', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1310210002', 'AG4',
     'Pesticide Dealer', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>A Pesticide Dealer may distribute restricted-use pesticides to another licensed Pesticide Dealer and/or to licensed Pesticide Applicators. They are responsible for selling only to those licensed with the correct applicator categories.  
Businesses selling general-use pesticides in volumes greater than 1 gallon liquid or 50 lb dry are also required to acquire a license to distribute.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>This is a business license required for all dealers wishing to distribute any restricted-use pesticides, or larger size containers of general-use pesticides.<br><h3>Fees</h3><br>License Fee- $25.00 per calendar year<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Renewal is required annually. The renewal fee is $25.00.',
     '0', '9', '9', '9', '9',
     '9', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'nda.nebraska.gov', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1310220000', 'LC1',
     'Alcohol Establishment', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>All businesses requesting to serve, sell, or allow the consumption of alcohol are required to obtain a liquor license issued by the Nebraska Liquor Control Commission.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>Applications must be filed with appropriate fees and required documentation before issuance of liquor licenses. <br><h3>Fees</h3><br>State & federal background checks via fingerprinting (per person)- $45.25 
Retail application fee- $400.00. Other classes vary.
License fees owed to the local governing body (city or county) vary depending on the class of license.<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Annual basic renewal fee- $45.00, or $145.00 with catering.
Remewal fees for other license classes vary.',
     '0', '0', '0', '0', '9',
     '0', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'lcc.nebraska.gov', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1310220001', 'FM2',
     'Fireworks Retail Stand Owner', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>A Retail Stand Owner is any person engaged in the business of making sales of fireworks at retail to consumers, or to persons other than distributors or jobbers.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>A complete application indicating the desired selling period must be submitted to the Nebraska State Fire Marshal’s office.
For licenses issued in an individuals name, rather than a business name, a Citizenship Attestation form is also required.<br><h3>Fees</h3><br>License Fee- $25.00<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Licenses are valid only during the selected selling period.',
     '0', '9', '9', '9', '9',
     '9', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'sfm.nebraska.gov', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1310310000', 'IN2',
     'Public Adjuster', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>Public adjuster means any person who, for compensation, performs any of the following:
Acts for or aids an insured in negotiating for or effecting the settlement of a first-party claim for loss or damage to real or personal property of the insured;
Advertises for employment as a Public Adjuster of first-party claims or otherwise solicits business or represents to the public that the person is a Public Adjuster of first-party claims for loss or damage to real or personal property of an insured;
Directly or indirectly solicits the business of investigating or adjusting losses or of advising an insured about first-party claims for loss or damage to real or personal property of the insured. <br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>Applicants must:
1. Be at least 18 years of age;
2. Have a principal place of residence or a principal place of business in Nebraska;
3. Have not committed any act that is grounds for denial pursuant to Neb. Rev. Stat. § 44-9211;
4. Have passed the Public Adjuster licensing examination; 
5. Be trustworthy, reliable, and of good reputation;
6. Have proof of financial responsibility ($20,000 Surety Bond)
7. Maintain an office in Nebraska with public access by reasonable appointment or regular business hours;
8. Have completed the Uniform Public Adjuster License Application.<br><h3>Fees</h3><br>Original License -$50.00  <br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Licenses expire on the last day of the individual''s birth month in the first year after issuance in which his or her age is divisible by two. Therefore, individuals born in even numbered years renew their license at the end of their birth month in the even numbered years and individuals born in odd numbered years renew their license at the end of their birth month in the odd numbered years. Licenses may be renewed beginning 90 days before their expiration dates. The renewal fee is $50.00. Resident public adjusters will be required to complete twenty-four (24) hours of continuing education to qualify for renewal, which must include three hours of ethics training.',
     '0', '9', '9', '9', '9',
     '9', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'doi.nebraska.gov', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1310410000', 'FM4',
     'Boiler/Pressure Vessel Inspector', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>A Boiler/Pressure Vessel Inspector inspects boilers, pressure vessels, piping, and other pressure retaining items, whether in service or during new construction. 
He or she uses state, national, and international codes to verify that the inspected items are in compliance with safety rules and construction standards. Some of the codes used are the National Board Inspection Code, the ASME Boiler and Pressure Vessel Codes, and the NFPA Codes. 
Following an inspection, he or she reports on the conditions of inspected items to the appropriate authority, makes recommendations for repair, and interfaces with the public, owners, and operators of inspected items.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>Boiler inspectors shall hold a valid commission with appropriate endorsements as issued by the National Board of Boiler and Pressure Vessel Inspectors and shall be employed by a National Board and/or ASME-accredited Authorized Inspection Agency, or by a National Board Member Jurisdiction. The National Board Commission is recognized in 48 of 50 states, all provinces of Canada, and in numerous other cities and countries around the world. Most jurisdictions issue reciprocal commissions to National Board Commissioned inspectors to permit them to inspect items within the jurisdiction. To obtain a National Board Commission, contact:The National Board of Boiler and Pressure Vessel Inspectors1055 Crupper AvenueColumbus, OH 43229-1183 Additionally, to inspect boilers in the state of Nebraska, a National Board Commissioned Inspector must also hold a valid Nebraska Commission, which can be obtained from the Chief Boiler Inspector upon submission of a citizen attestation, application, and payment of the $20.00 commission fee.<br><h3>Fees</h3><br>Nebraska Commission- $20.00 
National Board Commission- See website<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Nebraska Commissions must be renewed annually in December. The renewal fee is $20.00.',
     '0', '0', '0', '0', '0',
     '9', '0', '9', 'A', '0',
     'sfm.nebraska.gov', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1310740000', 'LB1',
     'Farm Labor Contractor', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>Farm labor contractors are individuals, partnerships, corporations, and/or cooperative associations which performany farm labor contracting activity for monetary or other valuable consideration. 
Farm labor contracting activities include recruiting, soliciting, hiring, employing, furnishing, and/or transporting of any migrant or seasonal agricultural worker.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>Businesses must submit an application, license fee, and surety bond (or equivalent) to the Nebraska Department of Labor.<br><h3>Fees</h3><br>License- $250.00, + $5,000 surety bond (or equivalent)<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Renewal is required annually by April 1. The renewal fee $250.00.',
     '0', '0', '0', '9', '9',
     '0', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'dol.nebraska.gov', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1320110000', 'PA1',
     'Accountant, Certified Public (CPA)', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>A Certified Public Accountant (CPA), who is certified and licensed, performs one or more kinds of servicesinvolving the use of accounting or auditing skills, including the issuance of reports on financialstatements which state or imply any level of assurance as to the reliability of any financial statementsassociated with such assurances; and/or performs one or more kinds of management advisory orconsulting services, or the preparation of tax returns, or the furnishing of advice on tax matters.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>Applicants must:1. Have earned a baccalaureate or higher degree from anaccredited institution(s) with 150 semester hours or 225quarter-hours of postsecondary academic credit;2. Have passed the Certified Public AccountancyExamination with a score of 75% or higher on each of thefour sections;3. Have passed the AICPA Ethics test with a score of 90%or higher;4. Meet one of the following:A. Have verification of 4,000 hours of accounting experienceover two years under the supervision of a licensedCPA in a licensed accounting firm.-OR-B. Have verification of 4,000 hours of accounting experience over two years in employment as anaccountant in the office of the Auditor of Public Accounts or the Department of Revenue.-OR-C. Have verification of 6,000 hours over three years, of accounting experience satisfactory to theBoard, in any state, in the employment as:(a) An accountant in government or business under the supervision of an active certified publicaccountant who is the holder of a permit issued under subdivision 1a of Neb. Rev. Stat. § 1-136or the equivalent issued by another state;-OR-(b) Faculty at a college or university of recognized standing under the supervision of an activecertified public accountant who is the holder of a permit issued under subdivision 1a of § 1-136or the equivalent issued by another state.<br><h3>Fees</h3><br>CPA Exam- $1,137.00Individual- $200.00Firm- $100.00Offices- $50.00<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Individuals renew biennially, with expiration on June 30.Firms renew annually.In order to renew an active permit for the following licensing period, licensees will be required to showthat they have taken a minimum of 80 continuing professional education hours, including a four-hourethics requirement, in the two preceding calendar years. CPE hours must be reported by January 31of each year.',
     '0', '0', '0', '0', '9',
     '0', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'nbpa.nebraska.gov', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1320210000', 'RV1',
     'County Assessor', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>County Assessors are elected officials of county government subject to re-election every four years. Their duties are set forth by Nebraska Revised Statute § 23-3201. 
County Assessors are responsible for valuing all real and personal property for purposes of taxation each year. The County Assessor is also responsible for administering various tax exemptions, preparing tax lists, and administrative reports for public record, complying with property laws, formulating operational policies, and directing assessment office activities. 
A complete list of duties can be found at the Nebraska Department of Revenue, Property Assessment Division’s website under “calendar.”<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>In order to obtain a certificate, applicants must sit for theCounty Assessor/Deputy County Assessor Exam. Thisexam is administered four times each year in February,May, August, and November. If there is a vacancy in office,an emergency exam may also be administered upon requestfrom the county Board. A grade of 70% or higher isrequired to pass.Within four years from the date of assuming the office, all County Assessors must complete IAAOCourse 101 (Fundamentals of Real Property Appraisal), and IAAO Course 300 (Fundamentals ofMass Appraisal). If a person has completed these required courses prior to taking office, he or shewill not be required to retake the courses, but must provide evidence of completion to the PropertyTax Administrator.<br><h3>Fees</h3><br>A $75.00 examination fee is required with an application to take the exam.<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>After obtaining a certificate, each person must complete at least 60 hours of approved continuing education during every four-year period.
Certificate renewal is automatic if the certificate holder has successfully completed the continuing education requirements. 
If the certificate holder has not completed 60 hours of continuing education, he or she may choose to instead retake the County Assessor/Deputy County Assessor exam within the last six months of their four-year certification period. If passed, the exam replaces the required continuing education and the certificate holder will remain active.',
     '0', '1', '1', '1', '0',
     '2', '2', '9', 'A', '0',
     'revenue.nebraska.gov/pad', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1320210001', 'RP1',
     'Real Property Appraiser', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>Real Property Appraisers develop an analysis, opinion, or conclusion relating to the specified interests in or aspects of identified real estate or identified real property. Real property appraisal includes, but is not limited to, evaluation assignments, valuation assignments, and appraisal review assignments. There are currently four levels of credentialing:. Certified General Real Property Appraiser- The scope of practice for the certified general real property appraiser shall include real property appraisal practice concerning all types of real property or real estate that appraiser is competent to engage in. . Certified Residential Real Property Appraiser- The scope of practice for a certified residential real property appraiser shall be limited to real property appraisal practice concerning residential real property or real estate having no more than four residential units, if any, without regard to transaction value or complexity. The appraisal of subdivisions for which a development analysis or appraisal is necessary is not included in the scope of practice for a certified residential real property appraiser. . Licensed Residential Real Property Appraiser- The scope of practice for a licensed residential real property appraiser shall be limited to real property appraisal practice concerning noncomplex residential real property or real estate having no more than four units, if any, with a transaction value of less than one million dollars and complex residential real property or real estate having no more than four units, if any, with a transaction value of less than four hundred thousand dollars. The appraisal of subdivisions for which a development analysis or appraisal is necessary is not included in the scope of practice for a licensed residential real property appraiser. . Trainee Real Property Appraiser- The scope of practice for the trainee real property appraiser shall be limited to real property appraisal practice assignments that the supervisory certified real property appraiser is permitted to engage in by his or her current credential and that the supervisory real property appraiser is competent to engage in.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>Certified General Real Property Appraiser-Education Prerequisites:1.  bachelor’s degree or higher from an accredited degree-awarding college or university;2. 300 hours qualifying education approved by the Board.Alternatively, a bachelor’s degree in Real Estate from an accredited degree-awarding college or university that has had all of part of its curriculum approved by the Appraiser Qualifications Board as required core curriculum may fulfill qualifying education requirements. Experience Prerequisites:3,000 hours of experience obtained in no fewer than 18 months, of which 1,500 hours must be in nonresidential appraisal work or successfully complete a PAREA program.  Examination:Within the twelve months following approval of the applicant''s education and experience by the Real Property Appraiser Board, pass a certified general real property appraiser examination. Certified Residential Real Property Appraiser-Education Prerequisites:1. Hold a bachelor''s degree, or higher, from an accredited degree-awarding college or university; or Hold an associate''s degree from an accredited degree-awarding community college, college, or university in the study of business administration, accounting, finance, economics, or real estate; orSuccessfully complete thirty semester hours of college-level education from an accredited degree-awarding community college, college, or university; orSuccessfully complete thirty semester hours of the College-Level Examination Program; orSuccessfully complete any combination of college-level education and College-Level Examination Programs that ensures coverage of all topics and hours identified. 2. 200 hours qualifying education approved by the Board.Alternatively, a bachelor’s degree in Real Estate from an accredited degree-awarding college or university that has had all of part of its curriculum approved by the Appraiser Qualifications Board as required core curriculum may fulfill qualifying education requirements. Experience Prerequisites:1,500 hours of experience obtained in no fewer than 12 months. Examination: Within the twelve months following approval of the applicant''s education and experience by the Real Property Appraiser Board, pass a certified residential real property appraiser examination or certified general real property appraiser examination.     Licensed Residential Real Property Appraiser- Education Prerequisites: 1. Hold a high school diploma or a certificate of high school equivalency or have education acceptable to the Real Property Appraiser Board. 2. 150 hours qualifying education approved by the Board. Experience Prerequisites: 1,000 hours of experience obtained in no fewer than 6 months. Examination: Within the twelve months following approval of the applicant''s education and experience by the Real Property Appraiser Board, pass a licensed residential real property appraiser examination, certified residential real property appraiser examination, or certified general real property appraiser examination. Trainee Real Property Appraiser- Education Prerequisites: 79-82 hours qualifying education approved by the Board. Experience Prerequisites: None. Examination:  None. <br><h3>Fees</h3><br>Application Fee - $150.00 Criminal History Record Check Fee - $45.25 New Credentials - $300.00 Credentialing Fee + $40.00 Federal Registry Fee for Certified General, Certified Residential, and Licensed Residential Real Property Appraisers<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Credential period begins on January 1 and expires on December 31 of the year in which it is issued. The renewal application must be filed on or before November 30. Credentials may be renewed for one or two years at a time. Certified General, Certified Residential, and Licensed Real Property Appraisers must pay the following fees to renew their credential: . Renewal Fee - $275.00 per year . Federal Registry Fee - $40.00 per year . Fingerprint Audit Program Fee - $5.00 per year Trainee Real Property Appraisers pay only a fingerprint audit program fee of $10.00 for a two-year renewal. Late renewal fees start on December 1 at a rate of $25.00 per month, including December. Renewals can be submitted until June 30 of the following year. Credential holders who fail to renew by the June 30 deadline must begin the process of application over as if applying for the first time. Twenty-eight hours of approved continuing education activities must be completed during each two-year continuing education period for any credential holder to qualify for renewal.',
     '0', '1', '2', '1', '0',
     '2', '0', '9', 'A', '0',
     'appraiser.ne.gov', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1320520000', 'BF3',
     'Investment Adviser', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>An Investment Adviser is a firm which engages in financial management and planning for individuals or otherwise advises others on securities. 
An Investment Adviser may be represented by an Investment Adviser Representative. A representative must be employed/registered by an Investment Adviser to transact business in Nebraska, unless exempt from registration.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>Registration requirements may be obtained by contacting the Nebraska Department of Banking and Finance.<br><h3>Fees</h3><br>Investment Adviser License Fee- $200.00<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Renewal is required annually by December 31. The renewal fee is $200.00.',
     '0', '9', '9', '9', '9',
     '9', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'ndbf.nebraska.gov', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1320520001', 'BF3',
     'Investment Adviser Representative', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>An Investment Adviser is a firm which engages in financial management and planning for individuals or otherwise advises others on securities. 
An Investment Adviser may be represented by an Investment Adviser Representative. A representative must be employed/registered by an Investment Adviser to transact business in Nebraska, unless exempt from registration.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>Registration requirements may be obtained by contacting the Nebraska Department of Banking and Finance.<br><h3>Fees</h3><br>Investment Adviser Representative License Fee- $40.00<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Renewal is required annually by December 31. The renewal fee is $40.00.',
     '0', '9', '9', '9', '9',
     '9', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'ndbf.nebraska.gov', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1320710000', 'SS1',
     'Credit Counselor', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>A Credit Counselor is an individual who helps individual debtors with debt settlement through education, budgeting, and the use of a variety of tools with the goal of reducing and ultimately eliminating debt. 
Credit counselors work for a Debt Management or Credit Services Organizations agency. The debt management or credit services organizations are issued a license to conduct business in Nebraska.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>Debt Management Agency: Applicants must submit an application, a blank copy of their contract they intend to use between the agency and the debtor, and a $10,000 surety bond.

Credit Services Organizations: Applicants must submit an application of registration, a blank copy of their contract, and a $100,000 surety bond or surety account. <br><h3>Fees</h3><br>Debt Management Agency:
Initial Fee- $200.00 
Investigation fee- $200.00
Branch Office- $100.00 per additional office
Credit Services Organization: 
Initial Fee- $100.00<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Debt Management Agency licenses must be renewed annually by December 1. The renewal fee is $100.00.
Credit Services Organization licenses do not expire qnd do not require payment of any additional renewal fees. Bond is typically renewed each year.',
     '0', '9', '9', '9', '9',
     '9', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'sos.nebraska.gov', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1320720000', 'BF1',
     'Credit Union Loan Officer', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>Credit Union Loan Officers evaluate, authorize, or recommend approval of commercial, real estate, or credit loans and advise borrowers on financial status and methods of payments. 
Mortgage Loan Officers & Agents, Collection Analysts, Loan Servicing Officers, and Loan Underwriters would all be considered Credit Union Loan Officers. 
Duties are potentially wide-ranging, but may also include exercising substantial employee supervision duties and/or duties assigned by a Manager or the Credit Committee.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>Applicants must submit an Application for Credit Union Loan Officer’s License to the Dept. of Banking & Finance.<br><h3>Fees</h3><br>License Fee-  $25.00<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>No Renewal Fee.',
     '0', '9', '9', '9', '9',
     '9', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'ndbf.nebraska.gov', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1320720001', 'BF2',
     'Mortgage Loan Originator', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>A Mortgage Loan Originator is any individual who for compensation or gain, or in the expectation of compensation or gain, takes a residential mortgage loan application, or offers or negotiates terms of a residential mortgage loan.
A Mortgage Loan Originator license is also required for individuals who solicit consumers to submit residential mortgage loan applications. Any Loan Processor or Underwriter who is an independent agent of a mortgage banker, mortgage registrant, or installment loan company must obtain a Mortgage Loan oOriginator license to conduct such processing or underwriting activity.
A Mortgage Loan Originator must be sponsored by a Nebraska-licensed Mortgage Banker to transact business in Nebraska.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>Applicants must: 1. Complete at least 22 hours of pre-license education, which must include at least the following:A. Three hours of instruction in federal law and regulations regarding mortgage loan origination;B. Three hours of instruction in ethics, which must include instruction on fraud, consumer protection, and fair lending issues;C. Two hours of instruction related to lending standards for the nontraditional mortgage product marketplace;D. Two hours of Nebraska-specific education;E. Twelve hours of elective courses; 2. Pass a written test developed by the Nationwide Mortgage Licensing System and Registry (NMLS).  The test must include the following subject areas:A. Ethics;B. Federal laws and regulations pertaining to mortgage origination;C. State laws and regulations, including instructions on fraud, consumer protection, the nontraditional mortgage marketplace, and fair lending issues.<br><h3>Fees</h3><br>Application Fees- $150.00Processing Fees- $30.00 Fingerprinting & Criminal Background Check- $36.25 if the applicant submits his or her fingerprints electronically; $46.25 if the applicant submits his or her fingerprints by paper A $15.00 fee for a credit report will also be added if one has not been authorized through NMLS in the past 30 days. <br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>',
     '0', '9', '9', '9', '9',
     '9', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'ndbf.nebraska.gov', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1320990000', 'IN2',
     'Viatical Settlement Broker', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>A Viatical Settlement Broker is a person, including a Life Insurance Producer, who, working exclusively on behalf of a viator and for a fee, commission, or other valuable consideration, offers or attempts to negotiate viatical settlement contracts between a viator and one or more viatical settlement providers or one or more Viatical Settlement Brokers.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>Applicants must hold a valid Life Insurance Producer license in the State of Nebraska and be at least 18 years of age. If the applicant does not hold an active life insurance license, then they must pass the viatical settlements exam and submit to the Department of Insurance evidence of financial responsibility in the amount of $250,000 through either a surety bond or a deposit of cash, certificates of deposit, or securities. <br><h3>Fees</h3><br>Original License -$40.00  <br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Licenses expire on the last day of the individual''s birth month in the first year after issuance in which his or her age is divisible by two. Therefore, individuals born in even numbered years renew their license at the end of their birth month in the even numbered years and individuals born in odd numbered years renew their license at the end of their birth month in the odd numbered years. Licenses may be renewed beginning 90 days before their expiration dates. The renewal fee is $40.00.  Resident viatical settlement brokers who are not life licensed will be required to complete fifteen (15) hours of continuing education to qualify for renewal, which must include three hours of ethics training.',
     '0', '1', '1', '1', '0',
     '2', '2', '9', 'A', '0',
     'doi.nebraska.gov', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1370710000', 'LB1',
     'Professional Employer Organization', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>A Professional Employer Organization is any person engaged in the business of providing professional employer services. These requirements are unaffected by use of terms like “staff leasing company,” “administrative employer,” “employee leasing company,” or any name other than Professional Employer Organization.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>Each individual, partnership, or corporation operating as a Professional Employer Organization must register with the Department of Labor. 
Applicants must complete a registration form and maintain working capital of at least $100,000.00. If positive working capital is less than $100,000.00, a bond, certificate of deposit, escrow account, or irrevocable letter of credit in that amount is required.<br><h3>Fees</h3><br>Full Registration- $250.00
Limited Registration- $100.00<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Renewal is required annually. Renewal fees are $250.00 for Full; $100.00 for Limited.',
     '0', '9', '9', '9', '9',
     '9', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'dol.nebraska.gov', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1710110000', 'EA2',
     'Architect', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>An Architect is a person who is licensed by the Board of Engineers & Architects to practice architecture.  Practice of architecture means providing or offering to provide design services in connection with the construction, enlargement, or alteration of a building or group of buildings and the space within and surrounding the buildings. The services may include, but not be limited to, planning, providing studies, designs, drawings, specifications, and other technical submissions, and administering construction contracts.  The practice of architecture does not include the practice of engineering. <br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>EducationA degree from either a National Architectural Accrediting Board (NAAB) or Canadian Architectural Certification Board (CACB) accredited program, OR meet the NCARB (National Council of Architectural Registration Boards) Education Standard.  Experience Completion of AXP or a NCARB-approved alternative for satisfactory completion of AXP. ExaminationSuccessfully pass the NCARB Architect Registration Exam (ARE).  Licensure by Reciprocity-Applicants who do not meet the above requirements may qualify for licensure upon presentation of satisfactory evidence of 15 years or more of licensed experience in compliance with the licensing laws in the jurisdictions where his or her architectural practice has occurred.To qualify for licensure by reciprocity, applicants must: 1. Have a valid NCARB certificate in good standing;- OR-2. Submit satisfactory evidence of 15 years of licensed work experience acceptable to the Board;- OR-3. Meet Nebraska’s initial license requirements. All reciprocal options require the applicant to apply to the Board, along with the required applica-tion fee; provide three references indicating good reputation and ethical character; and pass an examination on the statutes, rules, and other requirements unique to Nebraska.  <br><h3>Fees</h3><br>Fees are set by the Board and are subject to change. Check the Board’s website (ea.nebraska.gov/architect-fees) for current fees. License Application Filing Fee- $150 Architect Exam Application Filing Fee- $0 (additional testing fees required after approval by the Board. Such fees are determined by the exam administrator.)Â  <br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Licenses must be renewed biennially by December 31. The renewal fee is $80.00. Individuals with last names starting with the letters A through K renew in odd-numbered years; individuals with last names starting with the letters L through Z renew in even-numbered years.  Late Renewal Fee- 10% of biennial fee each month late, but late fees may not exceed the amount of the regular biennial fee. If a license has been expired for one year or more, a new application is required and the licensee must meet the current licensing requirements in place at the time of application.',
     '0', '0', '0', '0', '9',
     '0', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'ea.nebraska.gov', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1710120000', 'LA1',
     'Landscape Architect', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>No Change<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>A person applying for licensure as a professional landscape architect must submit an application and pay the required fees to  the Nebraska State Board of Landscape Architects. Applicants must meet the Council of Landscape Architectural Registration Boards (CLARB) Standards of Eligibility.Exceptions: If an applicant has not satisfied the Examination Standard of the CLARB Standards of Eligibility, but received their initial licensure by grandfather clause, is in good standing, and has a minimum of three years of experience at the time of the application to the Nebraska board, the examination requirement may be waived; orIf the applicant has a minimum of fifteen years of licensed experience in good standing, the examination and/or education requirements may be waived.<br><h3>Fees</h3><br>Initial License Application Fee: $100 (Individual has never been licensed and is seeking their very first license)Comity/Reinstatement License Application Fee: $300 (Individual is already licensed in at least one other jurisdiction and is seeking licensure in Nebraska for the first time or has been licensed in Nebraska previously and the license has been expired for over a year.)Examination Fees: Determined by the exam administrator<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Renewals are valid for two years.  Licensees with last names beginning with A-L renew in even-numbered years.  Those with last names beginning with M-Z renew odd-numbered years.Renewal Fee - $300.00 biennially.Late Renewal Fee- 10% of the annual fee each month late, but not to exceed the amount of the regularrenewal fee. After 1 year, a new application is required and the licensee must meet the currentlicensing requirements in place at that time.',
     '0', '0', '0', '0', '9',
     '0', '9', '9', 'A', '0',
     'nsbla.nebraska.gov', '20240906'),
    ('31', '01', '1710220000', 'LS1',
     'Land Surveyor, Registered', '<h3>Occupational Description</h3><br>Land Surveyors measure the distance, direction, and angle between two points and the elevation of points, lines, and contours on the earth’s surface. They research legal records and look for evidence of previous boundaries.Surveyors plan the fieldwork, select survey reference points, and determine the precise location ofany natural or constructed features in the survey project area.The surveyor will record the results of the survey and verify the accuracy of the data in addition topreparation of plats, maps, charts, and reports for legal documentation.<br><h3>Licensure Requirements</h3><br>The applicant must have at least six years of surveying experience, of which at least three must have been in a responsible position.Credit may be given for education that has been approved by the Board.<br><h3>Fees</h3><br>Application Fee- $40.00Registration Fee- $100.00<br><h3>Renewal</h3><br>Renewal is required biennially. The renewal fee is $100.00.Completion of 30 hours of professional development is required to qualify for renewal.',
