    ('32', '01', '17001', '170',
     'Collection Agency Manager', 'Collection agency managers are required to have a manager''s certificate.  
Applicants must:
1)  Be at least 21 years old and of good moral character.
2)  Have not been convicted of (or plead no contest to) a felony relating to the practice of collection agencies or agents or any crime involving fraud, misrepresentation or moral turpitude.
3)  Have not had a manager''s certificate suspended or revoked within the past 10 years.
4)  Have 2 years full-time experience collecting assigned accounts with a collection agency (one year of the required experience must have been obtained within the prior 18 months.)
5)  Pass an examination; examinations are given at least two times a year.  
6)  Submit 3 sets of fingerprints on FBI cards for a background check.

Note:  Renewal applications must be filed on or before June 1st each year.

Fees may be subject to change. Fees are:  application - $190; investigation--$115; initial certificate--$30; annual certificate renewal--$30; certificate reinstatement--$45.',
     '0', '1', '0', '9', '0',
     '2', '1', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://fid.state.nv.us', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17101', '171',
     'Mortgage Brokers, Bankers and Agents', 'Employees of mortgage brokers who conduct mortgage activities must be licensed as Mortgage Agents.  Applicants for mortgage agent licensure must meet the following requirements:
1)  Have not been convicted of, or entered a plea of, no contest to a felony within the preceding 7 years, or at any time if the felony involved fraud, dishonesty or a breach of trust or money laundering.
2) Have never had a license or registration as a Mortgage Agent, Mortgage Banker, Mortgage Broker or residential mortgage loan originator revoked in this State or any other jurisdiction or had a financial services license revoked within the immediately preceding 10 years.
3) Have not made a false statement of material fact on the application.
4) Have not violated any provision of NRS chapter 645B or 645E, a regulation adopted pursuant to those provisions, or an order of the Commissioner.
5) Have demonstrated financial responsibility, character and general fitness so as to command the confidence of the community and warrant a determination that s/he will operate honestly, fairly and efficiently for the purposes of Chapter 645B or Chapter 645E of NRS.
6)  Undergo a background check.
7) Have successfully completed pre-licensing and testing requirements.

Mortgage brokers and bankers must also be licensed in Nevada.  Visit the Division website for more information on the requirements for licensure. 
 
Fees may be subject to change.  Fees are: mortgage agent application--$185; agent annual renewal-- $165; exam fee --$60/hour; mortgage broker or banker application --$1,500; broker or banker license -- $1000 (first year) and $500 renewal.',
     '0', '9', '9', '9', '0',
     '9', '1', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://mld.nv.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17102', '171',
     'Escrow Agents', 'Escrow agencies and agents must be licensed in Nevada.   Escrow agents must be associated with an agency to hold a license.  Visit the Division website for information on the requirements.

Fees may be subject to change.  Fees are:  escrow agent application --$100; annual renewals --$125; exam - $60/hour; escrow agency application --$500; escrow agency license or annual renewal --$200.',
     '0', '1', '9', '9', '0',
     '9', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://mld.nv.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17301', '173',
     'Insurance Producers', 'Note:  The insurance industry designation for Agents is "Producers".
Applicants must:
1)  Be at least 18 years of age. 
2)  Complete pre-licensing education requirements at a state-approved school. 
3)  Pass the state insurance examinations (in the lines of authority applied for) administered by Pearson VUE.
4)  Provide fingerprint cards for a criminal history report.  
Note:  Producers applying to sell variable annuities or variable life must also be registered with the Financial Industry Regulatory Authority (FINRA) and the Nevada Secretary of State, Securities Division.

Visit the Division website for more information on licensing requirements.

Fees may be subject to change.  Fees are:  examination--$90; initial licensing--$195; business/agency affiliation--$60; fingerprinting --$37.50 (payable to Department of Public Safety) or $55.25 (electronic by Pearson VUE).',
     '0', '2', '1', '1', '0',
     '2', '4', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://doi.nv.gov#http://doi.nv.gov#', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17302', '173',
     'Bail Agent, Bail Solicitor and General Agent', 'Applicants must:
1)  Be a Nevada resident and at least 18 years old.
2)  Be employed by/affiliated with a bail business or appointed by an authorized surety company in Nevada.
3)  Have 6 hours of bail bond education approved by the Commissioner.
4)  Pass the Nevada Bail Exam administered by Pearson VUE.
5)  Be bondable (surety bond of $10,000 for solicitor, $25,000 for agent and $50,000 for general agent).
6)  Submit 2 fingerprint cards for the Division to obtain a criminal history report.
7)  Not have been convicted of a felony in Nevada (or an offense in another state that would be a felony in Nevada) or have been convicted of an offense involving moral turpitude or the illegal use, sale, or possession of a controlled substance.

Notes:  Bail Solicitors must work under the direction of a single bail agent.  The bail solicitor''s license must remain in the custody of the bail agent with whom he/she is employed.  Upon termination of employment, the bail agent must notify the Division of Insurance and return the license for cancellation.  

Visit the Division website for more information on the licensing requirements.

Fees may be subject to change.  Fees are:  examination--$90; licensing--$185 (triennial); business/agency affiliation--$50; fingerprinting --$37.50 (payable to Department of Public Safety) or $55.25 (electronic by Pearson VUE).',
     '0', '1', '1', '9', '0',
     '9', '1', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://doi.nv.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17303', '173',
     'Bail Enforcement Agent', 'Applicants must:
1)  Be at least 21 years old.
2)  Have a high school diploma, GED or equivalent.
3)  Have completed 80 hours of bail enforcement agent training approved by the Commissioner.
4)  Have First Aid in Emergencies and CPR Certification.
5)  Provide the results of a drug test, taken within 30 days of the application and administered by a certified lab.
6)  Provide the results of a psychological exam conducted by a psychiatrist or psychologist licensed in Nevada (the results must show no conditions that would adversely affect your ability to be a Bail Enforcement Agent).
7)  Pass the Nevada Bail Exam administered by Pearson VUE.
8)  Submit 2 fingerprint cards for the Division to obtain a criminal history report.
9)  Not have been convicted of a felony in Nevada (or an offense in another state that would be a felony in Nevada) or have been convicted of an offense involving moral turpitude or the illegal use, sale, or possession of a controlled substance.

Notes:  An applicant who is employed by a licensed bail agent may apply for a temporary license while completing the 80 hour training requirement.  A temporary license is valid until the training is completed or up to 9 months, whichever is sooner.

Visit the Division website for more information on the licensing requirements.

Fees may be subject to change.  Fees are:  examination--$90; licensing--$185 (triennial); business/agency affiliation--$50; fingerprinting--$37.50 (payable to Department of Public Safety) or $55.25 (electronic by Pearson VUE).',
     '0', '2', '1', '9', '2',
     '9', '1', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://doi.nv.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17304', '173',
     'Motor Vehicle Damage Appraiser', 'Applicants must be 21 years of age or older, obtain a $2,500 bond and submit 2 fingerprint cards for the Division to obtain a criminal history report.  

Visit the Division website for more information on the licensing requirements.

Fees may be subject to change.  Fees are:  licensing --$185; fingerprinting --$37.50 (payable to Department of Public Safety) or $55.25 (electronic by Pearson VUE).',
     '0', '9', '9', '9', '0',
     '9', '4', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://doi.nv.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17305', '173',
     'Claims Adjuster', 'Categories of licensure are independent or public adjusters, or associate adjusters.  Applicants for licensure as independent or public adjusters must:
1)  Be 18 years of age or older. 
2)  Have 2 years of experience handling claims and adjusting (within the past 5 years). 
3)  Pass the property and casualty exam administered by Pearson VUE.
4)  Have not been convicted of or plead guilty to certain crimes (see website below).
5)  Submit 2 fingerprint cards for a criminal history report.  

Associate adjusters must be 18 or older and an employee of a Nevada licensed adjuster.  They do not need to meet residency requirements and are not required to take the exam.

Visit the Division website for more information on the licensing requirements.

Fees may be subject to change.  Fees are:  licensing --$195; fingerprinting --$37.50 (payable to Department of Public Safety) or $55.25 (electronic by Pearson VUE).',
     '0', '1', '9', '9', '0',
     '2', '1', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://doi.nv.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17306', '173',
     'Title Agents and Escrow Officers', 'Title agents and escrow officers must be licensed in Nevada.   
Applicants must:
1)  Be a Nevada resident (or reside within 50 miles of the state) and at least 18 years of age. 
2)  Complete pre-licensing education requirements at a state-approved school.
3)  Provide proof of having at least one year of recent experience relating to title insurance that is acceptable to the Division.  
4)  Pass the state insurance examinations (in the lines of authority applied for) administered by Pearson VUE.
5)  Provide fingerprint cards for a criminal history report. 

Visit the Division website for more information on the licensing requirements.

Fees may be subject to change.  Fees are:  examination--$90; initial licensing--$195; business/agency affiliation--$60; fingerprinting --$37.50 (payable to Department of Public Safety) or $55.25 (electronic by Pearson VUE).',
     '0', '1', '9', '9', '0',
     '2', '4', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://doi.nv.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17501', '175',
     'Real Estate Broker', 'Categories of licensure are "broker" and "broker/salesperson".   Applicants for Real Estate Broker/Salesperson must have 64 college semester units combining real estate, business, economics, and other college-level courses and pass state and national examinations. Required education courses include 9 units of real estate principles, practices, law, and appraisal; 15 units of courses in business, economics, or other real estate topics (such as finance); 3 units of broker management training and 37 units of college courses other than those listed above. Active full time experience may be applied toward completion of the total educational requirement, with the exception of 18 hours of Nevada law and the 3 units of broker management training. (Two years of experience equals 16 semester units.)

Categories of Real Estate Broker license for those who wish to be licensed as a "broker of record" (a real estate broker who operates his/her own business) are "corporate," "partner," "individual," or "associate." In addition to the requirements specified for Real Estate Broker/Salesperson, applicants must submit a personal financial statement; must have been actively engaged as a full time licensed real estate broker/salesperson for at least 2 of the 4 years immediately prior to the issuance of the broker''s license; pass state and national examinations.

Continuing education hours are required for renewal of active status licenses. 

Fees may be subject to change. Fees are:  examination (standard)--$100; initial license--$105; license renewal--$180 (2-year). In addition to the initial license and license renewal fees there is a mandatory education, research, and recovery fund fee of $40 (initial) and $80 (renewal).',
     '0', '2', '2', '1', '0',
     '2', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://red.nv.gov#http://red.nv.gov#', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17502', '175',
     'Real Estate Salesperson', 'Applicants must have 90 hours or 6 semester credits of classroom instruction in real estate principles, practices and law from a Nevada Real Estate Commission accredited provider.  The course must include a minimum of 18 hours of Nevada law, consisting of  NRS 645, 119, 119A, 119B and their respective administrative codes.  Certified transcripts must be submitted with the license application.  Applicants must show proof of passing the national and Nevada state exams within one year of the application date.

Continuing education hours are required for renewal of active status licenses.  

Fees may be subject to change. Fees are:  examination (standard)--$100; initial license--$85; license renewal--$140 (2-year). In addition to the initial license and license renewal fees there is a mandatory education, research, and recovery fund fee of $40 (initial) and $80 (renewal).',
     '0', '1', '2', '1', '0',
     '2', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://red.nv.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17503', '175',
     'Residential Appraiser', 'Categories of licensure are "licensed" and "certified." 
Applicants for Licensed Residential Appraiser must:  
1)  Complete 153 hours of approved instruction.
2)  Obtain 2,400 hours of active appraising experience (as an intern or appraiser) in not less than 2 years.
3)  Pass the Appraisal examination (or show proof of active licensure in another state). 

Applicants for Certified Residential Appraiser must:  
1)  Have an Associate degree or higher, or at least 21 semester hours in approved subjects.
2)  Complete 203 hours of approved instruction.
3)  Obtain 2,500 hours of active, full time appraising experience (as an intern or appraiser) in not less than 2 years; 500 hours of which must include complex residential appraising as defined in NRS 645C.
4)  Pass the Appraisal examination (or show proof of active licensure in another state).

Real Estate Appraiser Intern:  Those wishing to enter the real estate appraising field without having met the above experience or education requirements may register with the Division as a Real Estate Appraiser Intern. Applicants must complete 78 hours of approved pre-licensing coursework prior to registering with the division.

Reciprocity:  Those holding a valid appraiser license from another state must:
1)  Submit the completed application (log and experience verification not required).
2)  Submit a copy of their license.
3)  Show proof of completing 3 hours of education in Nevada Appraisal Law (AZ applicants exempt).
4)  Provide a certified Letter of Good Standing from the licensing state. 

Fees may be subject to change. Fees are:  examination (standard)--$100; application--$100; license or certificate--$240; Federal Financial Institutions Examination Council fee--$80; biennial renewal --$320.   Registered Intern application--$100 and registration--$190 (valid 2 years).',
     '0', '1', '2', '1', '0',
     '2', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://red.nv.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17504', '175',
     'General Appraiser', 'Applicants for licensure as a Certified General Property Appraiser must:  
1)  Hold a bachelor''s degree or higher, or at least 30 semester credits in approved subjects. 
2) Complete 303 hours of approved instruction.
3)  Obtain 3,600 hours of active, full time appraising experience (as an intern or appraiser) in not less than 3 years; 1,500 hours of which must include experience appraising commercial real estate.
4)  Have passed the Appraisal examination (or show proof of active licensure in another state).

Fees may be subject to change. Fees are:  examination (standard)--$100; application--$100; general property appraiser license fee--$340; Federal Financial Institutions Examination Council fee--$80; biennial renewal --$420.',
     '0', '1', '2', '1', '0',
     '2', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://red.nv.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17505', '175',
     'Inspector of Structure', 'There are 3 categories of certification:  Residential, General and Master inspector.  Residential inspectors must:
1)  Have a high school diploma or the equivalent. 
2)  Have at least 40 hours of classroom instruction related to the inspection of structures that is approved by the Division. 
3)  Pass the examination.
4)  Demonstrate the ability to produce a complete and credible inspection report according to the standards and requirements of state laws and regulations. 
5)  Submit a log of having observed at least 25 inspections performed by a certified general or master inspector, or by an instructor approved by the Division.
6)  Submit proof of insurance coverage specified by the Division.
7)  Submit 2 fingerprint cards (or electronic verification issued from an approved vendor).

General inspectors must have had at least 50 hours and Master inspectors must have had at least 60 hours of approved classroom instruction.  Contact the Real Estate Division for more information.

Fees may be subject to change.  Fees are:  application--$100; exam-$100; certificate and renewal--$250; fingerprint cards-$38.25 (payable to Dept. of Public Safety).',
     '0', '1', '1', '1', '0',
     '2', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://red.nv.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17506', '175',
     'Licensed Energy Auditor', 'Applicants must:
1)  Have a high school diploma or the equivalent and be at least 18 years old. 
2)  Provide proof of certification or accreditation from an organization approved by the Division. 
3)  Provide proof of completing and passing at least 40 hours of related building science training and practice from a training provider approved by the Division.
4)  Submit proof of insurance coverage specified by the Division.
5)  Submit 2 fingerprint cards (or electronic verification issued from an approved vendor).

Fee:
Fees may be subject to change.  Fees are:  application--$350; fingerprint card processing--$38.25 (payable to Dept. of Public Safety); renewal--$250.',
     '0', '9', '9', '9', '9',
     '9', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://red.nv.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17601', '176',
     'Asbestos Abatement Contractors, Supervisors, Workers & Consultants', 'Applicants for a Contractor''s license must:
1)  Have 2 years of asbestos abatement experience.
2)  Provide proof of industrial insurance and a photo ID.
3)  Provide a copy of their Nevada State Contractor''s Board license.
4)  Provide proof of successful completion of a 5-day, EPA-approved training course for contractors* taken within the previous 12 months (or proof of certification of current refresher training if the initial training was more than 10 months prior to your application).

*Approved by the EPA pursuant to the Model Contractor Accreditation Plan.

Applicants for a Supervisor''s license must:
1)  Provide evidence of at least 4 months of experience supervising projects for asbestos abatement.
2)  Provide proof of successful completion of a 5-day, EPA-approved initial training course for asbestos abatement supervisor and a copy of certification of current refresher training if the initial training has expired.
3)  Provide a photo ID.

Applicants for a Worker''s license must:
1)  Provide proof of successful completion of a 4-day, EPA-approved initial training course for asbestos abatement worker and a copy of certification of current refresher training if the initial training has expired.
2)  Provide a photo ID.

Additional Information:  
The Division also issues licenses for asbestos abatement consultants in the following categories: inspector, management planner, project designer and project monitor.  Visit the Division website for more information on the requirements.  

Fees may be subject to change.  Fees are:  contractor''s license --$200; supervisor''s license --$50; worker''s license --$25; consultant license --$100.',
     '0', '9', '1', '9', '9',
     '2', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://dirweb.state.nv.us/OSHA/osha.htm', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17602', '176',
     'Elevator and Boiler Inspectors, Elevator Mechanics, Apprentices & Helpers', 'Elevator Mechanic, Mechanic''s Apprentice and Mechanic''s Helper:
Applicants must provide proof of successful completion of an approved training course for Elevator Mechanics.  Current refresher training certification is required if the initial training has expired, or documentation of required work history acceptable to the Division.  Contact the Division for more information.

Elevator and Boiler Inspectors:
Applicants must have current Qualified Elevator Inspector (QEI) certification or current National Board of Boiler and Pressure Vessel Inspectors (NB) certification.  Current refresher training certification is required if the initial training has expired.  Contact the Division for more information.

Fees are subject to change.  Fees are:  initial certificate --$100; renewal --$50.',
     '4', '0', '1', '9', '9',
     '9', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://dirweb.state.nv.us/OSHA/osha.htm', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17603', '176',
     'Mobile Crane and Tower Crane Operators', 'Certification is required to operate tower cranes or mobile cranes having a boom length of 25 feet or greater, or a maximum machine rated capacity of 15,000 pounds or greater.  Crane operators must be certified to operate the type of crane they are using.
The certification must:
1)  Be issued by an organization whose program of certification meets the criteria established by the Division and is accredited by the National Commission for Certifying Agencies, or an equivalent accrediting body approved by the Division.
2)  Certify that the applicant has met the standards to be a crane operator established by the American Society of Mechanical Engineers (standards B30.3, B30.4, or B30.5).

Fees:  Fees will vary by training provider/certifying organization used.  Contact the Division for more information.',
     '0', '1', '2', '9', '0',
     '2', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://dirweb.state.nv.us/OSHA/osha.htm', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17604', '176',
     'Photovoltaic Installers', 'Photovoltaic installers must be licensed through the Nevada Division of Industrial Relations.  Applicants must pass the exam administered by the Division.  Exams are by appointment only, contact the Division to schedule the test.  

Fees are subject to change.  Fees are:  initial license --$25; renewal --$25.',
     '0', '1', '0', '0', '0',
     '9', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://dirweb.state.nv.us/OSHA/osha.htm', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17701', '177',
     'Taxicab Driver', 'Except in Clark County, taxicab companies in Nevada are licensed by the state''s Transportation Authority (NTA).  The NTA does not license taxi drivers.  In general, those employed as taxi drivers (outside of Clark County) are required to have a valid driver''s license (Class C) and will be referred by their employer to a local law enforcement agency for a background check.  The requirements for taxicab drivers in Clark County are listed separately.',
     '0', '1', '0', '9', '0',
     '0', '4', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://www.tsa.nv.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17801', '178',
     'Taxicab Driver - Las Vegas Area', 'In the Clark County/Las Vegas area, taxi driver permits are issued by the Taxicab Authority.  To apply for a permit, applicants must be referred by their employer.  Applications must be completed in the Authority office and will not be allowed out.  Requirements include:
1)  Referral Sheet and Authorization of Information Sheet from a taxicab company;
2)  Valid Nevada Driver''s License (Class C);
3)  Proof of 30 days residency and addresses for the last 3 years;
4)  Last place of employment;
5)  Listing of all prior convictions and pending court cases on the application form;
6)  Ability to read, speak and understand English (electronic test given);
7)  Physical by a medical doctor (DOT physical);
8)  Completion of the Drivers Awareness Program (DAP); and 
9) Fingerprints for a background check.

Fees are subject to change.  Fees are:  application - $40; renewals, replacement or change of listed employer - $10; fingerprints - $51.25.',
     '0', '1', '0', '9', '0',
     '0', '4', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://taxi.nv.gov#http://taxi.nv.gov#', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17901', '179',
     'Elementary School Teacher', 'An elementary license authorizes the holder to teach kindergarten* through eighth grade (K-8).  
Applicants must have a bachelor''s degree from an accredited college or university and must have completed an approved program for elementary school teaching which included fifty semester hours of credit in specified subjects.  Visit the Department website for further information on requirements and course work.

*There is also a special license available for teaching birth through second grade.  Visit the Department website for specific information.

Reciprocity:
A license to teach in Nevada under state reciprocity may be issued under certain conditions.   

Fees are subject to change.  Fees are:  initial application -  $161; additional endorsement - $50.',
     '0', '9', '2', '9', '0',
     '0', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://teachers.nv.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17902', '179',
     'Secondary School Teacher', 'A secondary license authorizes the holder to teach grades 7-12.

Applicants must have a bachelor''s degree from an accredited college or university, and must have:
1)  Completed an approved program for secondary school teaching, or
2)  Completed required courses for a teaching major from an accredited institution and have 22 semester credits in professional secondary or occupational education approved by the Department.

Visit the Department website for further information on requirements and course work.

Reciprocity:
A license to teach in Nevada under state reciprocity may be issued under certain conditions.  

Fees are subject to change.  Fees are:  initial application -  $161; additional endorsement - $50.',
     '0', '0', '2', '9', '0',
     '0', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://teachers.nv.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17903', '179',
     'Speech Pathologist and Audiologist - School', 'Speech pathologists working in public schools must qualify for an endorsement through the Department of Education. Private practice speech pathologists and audiologists must be licensed through the Board of Speech Pathology and Audiology; those requirements are listed separately.

To receive an Endorsement for Teaching Pupils Who Have Speech and Language Impairments (pupils ages 3-21) the applicant must:
1)  Have completed a program of preparation for teaching pupils who have speech and language impairments, which has been approved by the Department or;
2)  Hold a certificate of clinical competence in speech from the American Speech and Hearing Association or equivalent license from the Board of Examiners for Audiology and Speech Pathology or;
3)  Hold a current license or certificate, issued by another state, with an endorsement to teach pupils who have speech and language impairments or;
4a)  Hold a bachelor''s or master''s degree in the area of speech and language impairments and have 8 semester hours of student teaching, a clinical practicum, equivalent field experience, or 1 year of verifiable teaching experience with pupils who have speech and language impairments; or
4b)  Have completed a bachelor''s or master''s degree and completed specific course work stipulated by the Department.

To receive an endorsement to provide audiological services for pupils ages 3-21, a person must:
1)  Have completed a teaching program approved by the Department, or
2)  Hold a license or certificate from another state with an endorsement to teach hearing impairments, or
3)  Hold a certificate from the Council on Education of the Deaf, or
4)  Hold a bachelor''s of Master''s degree in the area of hearing impairments and have completed 8 semester credits of student teaching, equivalent field experience or 1 year of verifiable teaching experience in special education, or
5)  Hold a bachelor''s or master''s degree and complete the coursework specified by the Department.

Note:  An endorsement is not required to provide school audiological services.

\b+~Fees\b-~
Fees are subject to change.  The initial application fee is $161.',
     '3', '2', '2', '1', '2',
     '2', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://teachers.nv.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17904', '179',
     'Counselor - School', 'School counselors must have an elementary or secondary counseling endorsement.

To qualify for an endorsement to serve as an elementary or secondary school counselor, a person must:
1)  Hold a master''s or more advanced degree in school counseling, or
2)  Hold a specialty credential as a National Certified Counselor issued by the National Board for Certified Counselors, or  
3)  Hold a master''s or a more advanced degree with a major in counseling and meet the additional counseling experience and graduate coursework requirements specified by the Department, or  
4)  Hold a master''s or a more advanced degree, and have at least 2 years of teaching or school counseling experience, and meet the additional counseling experience and graduate coursework requirements specified by the Department.

Visit the Department website for more information on the additional counseling experience and coursework referred to in items 3 and 4.

Fees are subject to change.  The initial application fee is $161.',
     '0', '0', '2', '9', '1',
     '2', '0', '9', 'D', '0',
     'http://teachers.nv.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17905', '179',
     'Psychologist - School', 'Psychologists employed in schools must obtain an endorsement from the Department of Education. (Private practice psychologists must be licensed through the Board of Psychological Examiners. Requirements for private practice psychologists are listed separately.)

To qualify for an endorsement to serve as a school psychologist, a person must:
1)  Hold a graduate degree from an accredited college or university with a concentration in school psychology and;
2)  Have successfully completed the course work and earned the graduate credits required by the Department and;
3)  Have completed an internship in school psychology as outlined in the Department''s practicum requirements.
Or, a person must:
4)  Hold a certificate as a nationally certified school psychologist issued by the National School Psychology Certification System of the National Association of School Psychologists, or
5)  Have completed a program approved by the Department which prepares a person to be a school psychologist.

Fees are subject to change.  The initial application fee is $161.',
     '0', '2', '2', '9', '1',
     '2', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://teachers.nv.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17906', '179',
     'Librarian - School', 'To receive an endorsement as a school Library Media Specialist, a person must have a valid elementary, middle school or junior high, secondary or special teaching license (excluding a business and industry or special qualifications), and must:
1)  Have completed a program for school Library Media Specialists which has been approved by the Department or a regional accrediting association, or
2)  Hold a master''s degree in library science with specialization in school librarianship from a school accredited by the American Library Association; or
3)  Have completed 21 semester hours of course work specified by the Department.

Fees are subject to change.  The initial application fee is $161; additional endorsement - $50.',
     '0', '9', '2', '9', '0',
     '0', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://teachers.nv.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17907', '179',
     'School or Program Administrator', 'The Nevada Department of Education issues the following types of administrative endorsements:
1)  Professional administrator of a school.
2)  Professional administrator of a program.
3)  Supervisor of curriculum and instruction.

A person holding an endorsement as an administrator of a school may be employed by a school district as a superintendent, principal, assistant superintendent, vice principal, administrative assistant, supervisor, or coordinator of various programs.  An endorsement as an administrator of a program is necessary to supervise or coordinate a program of:  nursing, psychology (as it is applied in a school), speech therapy, physical therapy or occupational therapy.  Visit the Department website for specific licensure and coursework requirements.

Fees are subject to change.  The initial application fee is $161.  An additional endorsement is $50.',
     '0', '9', '9', '9', '0',
     '0', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://teachers.nv.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17908', '179',
     'Exceptional Pupils Endorsements', 'The Nevada Department of Education issues the following types of endorsements for licensed* teachers to teach exceptional pupils at various grade levels:  Adapted Physical Education, Autism, Early Childhood Developmentally Delayed, (Limited) Generalist, Gifted and Talented, Hearing Impairments, Intellectual Disabilities, Speech and Language Impairments, Visual Impairments, and Orientation and Mobility.  

*See the licensure requirements for elementary and secondary teachers.

Visit the Department website for information on specific endorsement requirements.

Fees are subject to change.  The initial application fee is $161.  An additional endorsement is $50.',
     '0', '9', '9', '9', '0',
     '9', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://teachers.nv.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '17909', '179',
     'Middle School and Junior High Teacher', 'A middle school or junior high school license authorizes the holder to teach grades 7-9*.
Applicants must have a bachelor''s degree from an accredited college or university, and must have:
1)  Completed an approved program for middle school or junior high teaching, or
2)  Completed 24 semester credits in professional education approved by the Department, and
3)  Completed 24 semester credits in a major field of endorsement or area of concentration.

* Note:  The middle school/junior high license does not qualify one to teach 9th grade in a high school setting.

Visit the Department website for further information on requirements and course work.

Reciprocity:
A license to teach in Nevada under state reciprocity may be issued under certain conditions.  

Fees are subject to change.  Fees are:  initial application -  $161; additional endorsement - $50.',
     '0', '9', '2', '9', '0',
     '0', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://teachers.nv.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '18101', '181',
     'Well Driller', 'Well drillers must be licensed by the Water Resources Division of the Department of Conservation and Natural Resources to operate in Nevada. (Note:  Contractor licensing for Well Drilling and Pump Installation (C-23) is listed separately.  C-23 licensing is required if you own a drilling rig.)

Applicants must have at least two years of full-time well drilling experience, submit an application with references and child support information, and pass a three-part examination given in the following order:
1)  A written part to determine knowledge of the well drilling rules and regulations (a score of at least 80% is required).
2)  Identification of a well location on a topographic map by a full legal description.
3)  An oral interview to determine drilling experience and knowledge of drilling techniques. 
Each part of the examination must be successfully completed to qualify to take the next part and receive a license. The examination is given once every three months in Carson City and Las Vegas.

Continuing education credits are required for license renewal.

Fees may be subject to change. Fees are:  application--$100; annual license renewal--$50.',
     '0', '1', '0', '1', '0',
     '2', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://water.nv.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '18301', '183',
     'Alcohol and Drug Abuse Counselor', 'Alcohol and Drug Abuse Counselors must be licensed or certified in the state of Nevada.  Applicants for licensure or certification must: 
1)  Be at least 21 years old. 
2)  Not have abused alcohol or controlled substances for 2 years prior to submitting an application.
3)  Not have been on parole or probation for 2 years prior to submitting an application.
4)  Observe "Ethical Standards and Requirements".
5)  Submit 2 sets of fingerprint cards.  
In addition to the general requirements listed above, specific requirements for each category are listed below. 

Certified Counselor Intern (CADC-Intern):  a bachelor''s degree or 60 credits towards a bachelor''s degree.  

Certified Alcohol and Drug Abuse Counselor (CADC):  a bachelors degree in an approved social science field from an accredited college or university; 2 years of full time employment (4,000 hours) under clinical supervision in alcohol and drug abuse counseling;  a written exam (scoring at least 70% to proceed to the oral exam), and an oral exam (scoring at least 70% to pass).   

Licensed Alcohol and Drug Abuse Counselor (LADC):  a Masters degree in an approved social science field in addition to those requirements listed above for a CADC.  

For information regarding exceptions to the certification/licensure requirements, recertification/continuing education requirements and/or substituting education in lieu of experience requirements, visit the Board''s website.

Fees may be subject to change.  Fees are:  CADC Intern application/license- $210; CADC application/license- $470 (includes $60 processing fee and $280 testing fees); LADC application/license- $490 (includes $60 processing fee and $300 testing fees); renewal (valid 2 years)-- $250; intern certification renewal --$75 annually.',
     '0', '1', '2', '1', '9',
     '2', '0', '9', 'D', '0',
     'http://alcohol.nv.gov#http://alcohol.nv.gov#', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '18302', '183',
     'Problem Gambling Counselor', 'Problem Gambling Counselors must be certified in the state of Nevada.  Applicants for certification must: 
1)  Be at least 21 years old. 
2)  Not have abused alcohol or controlled substances for 2 years prior to submitting an application.
3)  Not have been on parole or probation for 2 years prior to submitting an application.
4)  Observe "Ethical Standards and Requirements".
5)  Submit 2 sets of fingerprint cards.   
In addition to the general requirements listed above, specific requirements for each category are listed below.

Certified Problem Gambling Counselor:  Bachelors, Masters or PhD in an approved social science field, 2,000 hours employment under clinical supervision in problem gambling counseling, proof of completion of 60 hours of training specific to problem gambling approved by the Board, and pass a written exam (scoring at least 70%) and oral interview.

Certified Problem Gambling Counselor Intern:  a high school diploma or GED, verification of graduation from an accredited college or university in an appropriate social science field or submittal of an educational affidavit indicating enrollment in college, a supervision agreement, and proof of completion of 30 hours of training specific to problem gambling approved by the Board.

Problem Gambling Supervisor''s Certificate:  Visit the Board''s website for information on the requirements.

Allowance for Licensure by Other Nevada State Boards:  one licensed as a clinical social worker, nurse, marriage and family therapist, psychologist, or alcohol and drug abuse counselor may apply for Problem Gambling Counselor licensure if (s)he has:
1)  Completed at least 6 months or 1,000 hours of supervised counseling of problem gamblers approved by the board, and 
2)  Completed at least 60 hours of training specific to problem gambling approved by the board.

For information regarding exceptions to the certification/licensure requirements, recertification/continuing education requirements and/or substituting education in lieu of experience requirements, visit the Board''s website.

Fees may be subject to change.  Fees are:  problem gambling counselor application/certificate - $620 (includes $60 processing fee and $410 testing fees); renewal (valid 2 years)-- $250; problem gambling counselor intern certificate - $225; intern certification renewal--$75 annually; problem gambling supervisor''s certificate - $60.',
     '0', '1', '2', '1', '9',
     '2', '0', '9', 'D', '0',
     'http://alcohol.nv.gov#http://alcohol.nv.gov/#', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '18501', '185',
     'Emergency Medical Technician', 'All levels of emergency medical technicians (EMTs) must be certified. In Nevada, the requirements for certification apply to those individuals not working in Clark County.  Certification requirements for emergency medical technicians in Clark County are regulated by Clark County laws and are listed separately.

Applicants for EMT certification must:
1)  Be at least 18 years old.
2)  Be able to speak and write in English.
3)  Complete a 110-hour course developed by the U.S. Dept. of Transportation and approved by the Health Division.
4)  Pass a written and practical examination. 
5)  Have a certificate to provide cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) issued by the American Heart Association or the equivalent approved by the Health Division.

Advanced applicants must meet the basic requirements and have additional specialized training in IV (intravenous) therapy, IO (Intrasseous) infusion, medication administration, and advanced airway management techniques.  Applicants must pass the advanced-level exams.

Paramedic applicants must meet the basic requirements, have additional specialized training, have a certificate in Advanced Cardiac Life Support (ACLS), and pass the advanced-level exams. 

Advanced and Paramedic EMTs must be licensed as ambulance attendants and may only perform their skills as part of a permitted ambulance service or fire agency response team.

Continuing education and skills testing is required for certificate renewal.  Contact the Division for additional information. 

Fees may be subject to change. EMT, Advanced or Paramedic EMT certificate (Nevada resident)--$10; renewal-- $10; late fee for renewal --$10.  Ambulance and fire agency attendant license--$10 and fingerprints -- $37.50.  Out-of-state residents may apply for reciprocal certification. Fee for reciprocal certificate EMT --$40; Advanced --$50; Paramedic --$60.',
     '0', '3', '1', '1', '2',
     '2', '2', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://dpbh.nv.gov/Reg/EMS/EMS-home/', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '18701', '187',
     'Laboratory Director', 'There are three laboratory categories for director licensure:  Licensed, Registered  and Pulmonary Laboratory Director.

To apply for licensure as a Director of a LICENSED laboratory, an applicant must: 
1)  Be a physician licensed by the Nevada State Board of Medical Examiners to practice medicine in the state and meet additional related certification, training or experience requirements set by the Division, OR
2)  Hold a doctoral degree from an accredited institution with a major in a chemical, physical, or biological science, and be certified by one or more of the following American Boards of:  Medical Microbiology, Clinical Chemistry, Bioanalysis, Medical Laboratory Immunology, Forensic Toxicology, or Medical Genetics (or have qualifications equivalent to certification.)

An applicant for licensure as a director of a licensed laboratory for pulmonary conditions must be a physician licensed to practice in Nevada and must be certified by the American Board of Internal Medicine in the subspecialty of pulmonary disease.

To apply for licensure as a Director of a REGISTERED laboratory, an applicant must:
1)  Be a physician licensed by the Nevada State Board of Medical Examiners to practice medicine in the state and:
a)  Have at least 1 year of experience directing or supervising non-waived laboratory testing in an approved laboratory; or
b)  Have at least 20 credit hours of continuing medical education in laboratory practice regarding the responsibilities of a director; or
c)  Have received approved laboratory training during medical residency equivalent to the 20 hours of continuing medical education mentioned above; 
OR
2)  Hold a doctoral degree from an accredited institution with a major program of study in a chemical, physical, or biological science and:
a) have 1 year of experience directing or supervising laboratory testing in an approved laboratory; or
b)  Be certified by one or more of the following American Boards of:  Medical Microbiology, Clinical Chemistry, Bioanalysis, Medical Laboratory Immunology, Forensic Toxicology, or Medical Genetics (or have qualifications equivalent to certification.)

Fees may be subject to change. Fees are:  initial licensure--$500; biennial renewal --$300.',
     '3', '0', '2', '1', '2',
     '2', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://dpbh.nv.gov/Reg/RegulatoryPgms/', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '18702', '187',
     'Laboratory Technologist (Various)', 'The Nevada State Health Division, Bureau of Health Care Quality and Compliance issues certificates for several laboratory technology occupations including Clinical Laboratory Technologist, Cytotechnologist, Histotechnologist, and Blood-Gas Technologist. 

Applicants for a CLINICAL LABORATORY TECHNOLOGIST certificate must:
1a) Complete a bachelor''s degree in medical technology from an accredited institution and pass a national certification exam approved by the Board, or
  b) Complete a bachelor''s degree in one of the chemical, physical, or biological sciences at an accredited institution, and have at least 1 year of additional full-time experience or training in the specialty or subspecialty in which (s)he performs tests, and pass a national certification examination approved by the board, or
  c) Have passed the clinical laboratory technologists exam given by the U.S. Dept. of Health and Human Services.

Applicants for a CYTOTECHNOLOGIST certificate must complete 2 years of course work in an accredited institution with at least 12 semester hours in science, 8 hours of which are in biology, and complete at least 12 months of training in an accredited school of cytotechnology; or, meet the requirements for an equivalent combination of education and experience.  Applicants must pass a national certification examination.

Applicants for a HISTOTECHNOLOGIST certificate must complete a bachelor''s degree from an accredited institution, with 32 semester hours in science that includes 12 hours in chemistry and 16 hours in biology, histology, zoology, anatomy and physiology, and have 1 year of experience in a histopathology laboratory under the supervision of a board-certified pathologist; or, meet the requirements for an equivalent combination of education and experience. 

Applicants for a BLOOD-GAS TECHNOLOGIST (Respiratory) certificate may qualify by obtaining one of the following credentials:
1)  National Board for Respiratory Care certification as a certified respiratory therapist, or as a registered respiratory therapist. (Note that Respiratory Therapists are profiled separately and are licensed by the Board of Medical Examiners).

Laboratory Technologists may also obtain an additional certificate in one or more of the following specialty areas by meeting specified education and certification requirements. Specialty areas include chemistry, microbiology, hematology, immunology, immunohematology, nuclear medicine, histocompatibility, histology, cytology, and biotechnology.

Provisional certificates will be issued for those licenses requiring an applicant to pass a national certification examination if the applicant has been accepted as a candidate for testing and has shown that they meet all other requirements. The provisional certificate expires 180 days from the issue date.

The Bureau does not administer the national tests to applicants, but does accept results of national examinations given by the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services or other approved national agencies or boards.  Contact the Bureau for information on the approved exams.

Fees may be subject to change. Fees are:  initial certification--$113; certification renewal--$75.',
     '0', '2', '2', '1', '0',
     '2', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://dpbh.nv.gov/Reg/RegulatoryPgms/', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '18703', '187',
     'Laboratory Technician and Assistant (Various)', 'The Nevada State Health Division, Bureau of Health Care Quality and Compliance issues certificates for several laboratory technician and laboratory assistant occupations including Medical Technician, Histologic Technician, Blood-Gas Technician, Pathologist''s Assistant, Laboratory Assistant, and Office Laboratory Assistant (OLA).

Applicants for a MEDICAL TECHNICIAN certificate must have an associate degree as a medical technician or complete a program based on a course of study from an accredited institution including chemistry, biology, and laboratory techniques; or, meet the requirements for an equivalent combination of education and experience (NAC 652.440). Applicants must pass a national examination for certification.

Applicants for a HISTOLOGIC TECHNICIAN certificate must complete a program certified by the Committee on Allied Health Education and Accreditation in histotechnology; or, meet the requirements for an equivalent combination of education and experience (NAC 652.437).

Applicants for a  PATHOLOGIST''S ASSISTANT certificate must successfully complete a training program approved by the American Association of Pathologists'' Assistants; or, possess a bachelor''s degree with a major or minor in a biological science or an allied health field and have at least 3 years of experience in surgical pathology and autopsy pathology (NAC 652.452).

Applicants for a BLOOD-GAS TECHNICIAN (Respiratory)  certificate must complete a 1- or 2- year program from an AMA approved school of respiratory therapy or cardiopulmonary technology, or complete an equivalent course of training in the theory and practice of the determination of blood gases (NAC 652.447).

LABORATORY ASSISTANTS are phlebotomists who work in an independent licensed lab. Applicants must be high school graduates (or have a GED) and have 6 months of training in a laboratory approved by the Bureau; or, complete a qualifying course to take the phlebotomy certification exam administered by the American Medical Technologists, the American Society of Clinical Pathologists, or the National Certification Agency, and pass the exam.

OFFICE LABORATORY ASSISTANTS are phlebotomists and medical assistants who work in a  physician''s private practice (includes MAs, LPNs, RNs and CNAs). 

PHLEBOTOMISTS  working in a hospital must have a Lab Assistant certificate.  Phlebotomists working in a doctor''s office must have an Office Laboratory Assistant certificate. 

Technicians may also obtain an additional certificate in one or more of the following specialty areas by meeting specified education and certification requirements. Specialty areas include chemistry, microbiology, hematology, immunology, immunohematology, nuclear medicine, histocompatibility, histology, autopsy assistant, and biotechnology.

The Bureau does not administer the national tests to applicants, but does accept results of national examinations given by the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services or other approved national agencies or boards.  Contact the Bureau for more information on the approved exams.

Fees may be subject to change. Fees are:  initial certification, technician--$113; initial certification, laboratory assistant/OLA--$60; initial certification, pathologist''s assistant--$113; certification renewal, technician--$75; certification renewal, laboratory assistant/OLA--$45; pathologist''s assistant--$75.',
     '0', '2', '2', '1', '0',
     '2', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://dpbh.nv.gov/Reg/RegulatoryPgms/', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '18801', '188',
     'Mammography Technologist', 'Applicants for certification must:
1)  Have valid general radiographer credentials from the American Registry of Radiologic Technologists (ARRT) or another organization approved by the Division.
2)  Provide proof of advanced training in mammography (at least 40 hours) accredited by a recognized credentialing institution such as ASRT or ARRT.  Contact the Division for further information on approved training.

Additional Notes:  If you have been mammography qualified for over 3 years, you must provide proof of at least 15 CEU''s in mammography obtained within the prior 3 years.

Fees may be subject to change.  Fees are:  application --$88.',
     '0', '0', '2', '1', '0',
     '9', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://health.nv.gov/', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '18901', '189',
     'Environmental Health Specialist', 'Environmental Health Specialists must register with the board.
Applicants for registration must: 
1a)  Have at least a bachelor''s degree in environmental health, environmental health sciences, or have a Master''s degree in Public Health from an accredited institution; or
1b)  Have at least a bachelor''s degree which included 30 semester credits (45 quarter credits) of course work in core natural science (such as biology, chemistry, physics, geology or others as approved by the board), as well as sanitary or environmental engineering, and have at least 2 years of experience approved by the board in the field of public health; or
1c)  Have training or experience obtained during U.S. or Nevada state military service that is equivalent to 2 years of experience in environmental health as determined by the Board.
2)  Submit fingerprint cards for a background check.
3)  Pass the National Environmental Health Association (NEHA) examination.

Environmental Health Specialist Trainees are required to register within 90 days of becoming employed.  They must work under the supervision of a Registered Environmental Health Specialist (REHS) and pass the exam within 3 years of being hired.

Fees may be subject to change. Fees are:  new regular and new trainee fees - $395 (includes processing - $50, NEHA REHS exam - $125, Pearson Vue fee--$100; background check - $55, and annual registration --$65); registration by reciprocity--$170 (includes processing - $50, background check - $55, and annual registration - $65).',
     '0', '2', '2', '9', '0',
     '2', '4', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://nvrehs.org', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '19101', '191',
     'Commercial Driver''s License', 'Different classes of commercial drivers'' licenses are issued by the Department of Motor Vehicles (DMV) depending on the number of people being transported and/or the size of the vehicle being driven.

People who drive commercial vehicles weighing 26,001 or more pounds must have a Class A or Class B commercial driver''s license. The type of license depends on whether the vehicle is a combination type such as a tractor-trailer (Class A) or a single type such as a dump truck or a bus (Class B). People who drive vehicles to transport hazardous materials or drive vehicles weighing 26,000 pounds or less designed to transport 16 or more passengers, including the driver, must have a Class C commercial driver''s license. 

Types of endorsements that may be added to the commercial driver''s license include the following:  double/triple trailers (T); passenger transport (P); school bus driver (S) (also requires a (P) endorsement); tank vehicle (N); hazardous materials (H); and hazardous materials and tank vehicle (X). Applicants must pass a different knowledge test for each endorsement. A skills test is also required to add a passenger transport (P) endorsement or a school bus driver (S) endorsement.

To obtain a CDL, applicants must be at least 21 years old for interstate transport or for a passenger (P) or hazardous materials (H) endorsement.  Applicants who are 18-20 years old may be issued a restricted license (no H or P transport) that is valid for in-state transport only.  All applicants must pass written, vision, and driving skills examinations.  Additional knowledge and skills tests must be passed for each endorsement to be added to the commercial driver''s license.  If qualified, members of the Armed Forces may submit a form to have the skills test waived (if federal guidelines are met).  A medical examination is may be required based on the drivers self-certification at the time of licensing.

Fees may be subject to change. Fees are:  initial or transfer CDL (including all tests) - $142.25; with knowledge test only or no test - $112.25; learners permit - $58.25;  additional endorsement - $14; driving skills test - $30.',
     '0', '1', '0', '9', '9',
     '0', '0', '2', '9', '0',
     'http://dmvnv.com', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '19201', '192',
     'Motor Vehicle Salesperson', 'Vehicle salespersons and managers must be licensed.  Applicants must submit the application packet, a waiver form for a criminal history check, two sets of fingerprints (on cards supplied by the Department) and the certificate of employment form completed by a licensed Nevada vehicle dealer.

Fees are subject to change.  Fees are:  license --$75; annual renewal --$40; transfer fee --$20.',
     '0', '9', '9', '9', '0',
     '9', '4', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://dmvnv.com', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '19301', '193',
     'Pilots and Flight Engineers', 'Pilots and flight engineers must hold appropriate FAA certificates. Scheduled airline captains or pilots need an airline transport certificate.  Pilots in general aviation who fly for hire and copilots for commercial airlines need a commercial pilot certificate with instrument privileges.  Flight engineers need a flight engineer certificate.  

In general, the following requirements apply, however, contact the FAA for specific certification requirements.

All pilots who are paid to transport passengers or cargo must have a commercial pilot''s certificate.  To qualify for a commercial pilot''s certificate, applicants must:
1)  Be at least 18 years old.
2)  Have at least 250 hours of flight experience.
3)  Pass a physical exam.
4)  Have 20/20 vision with or without glasses.
5)  Have good hearing.
6)  Be able to read, speak, write and understand English.
7)  Pass the required written exams.
8)  Demonstrate their flying abilities to FAA examiners.

To qualify for an airline transport pilot''s certificate, applicants must meet the above requirements plus:  be at least 23 years old, have a minimum of 1,500 hours of flight experience, and pass written and flight exams.

Advanced ratings are available on both types of pilots'' certificates.  To achieve an advanced rating, pilots must take additional hours of instruction and pass exams.  

All certificates remain valid as long as pilots can pass the periodic physical exams and tests of flying skills.',
     '5', '1', '9', '9', '0',
     '2', '0', '2', '9', '0',
     'http://www.faa.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '19302', '193',
     'Aircraft Mechanic', 'The Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) certifies aircraft mechanics.  Mechanics must be at least 18 years old, be able to read, speak, write and understand English, and pass written, oral and practical exams.  The FAA requires graduation from an FAA-approved school or at least 18 months of work experience for an airframe or power plant certificate. For a combined certificate, an applicant must be a graduate of an approved school or have at least 30 months of experience working with both engines and airframes.  The FAA requires on-going training for those who have an airframe and powerplant certificate. 

Aircraft repairmen are also certified by the FAA.  The certificate is issued for specific tasks performed under supervision (such as in FAA-approved repair stations). 

To obtain an inspector’s authorization, a mechanic must have held an airframe and power plant certificate for at least three years.  Visit the FAA website for more information.',
     '5', '1', '2', '9', '9',
     '2', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     'http://www.faa.gov', '20160114'),
    ('32', '01', '19701', '197',
     'Repossessor', 'Applicants must:
1)  Be at least 21 years old and of good moral character.
2)  Undergo a background check through FBI and DPS.
3)  Have no criminal or felony convictions involving moral turpitude or illegal use or possession of a dangerous weapon.
4)  Have 5 years of repossession experience certified by their employer (2,000 hours each year; 10,000 total).
5)  Pass a State Board examination with a score of 75% or better.
6)  Obtain at least $200,000 worth of liability insurance. 

In addition to the fees listed below, a background investigation deposit fee is also required and is applied to the cost of conducting an investigation. The fee is $750 for the first license category and $250 for each additional license category.

Fees may be subject to change. Fees are:  application processing --$20; examination--$100 (nonrefundable); abeyance fee--$100 per license per year; initial license--license fee is prorated; annual license renewal--$500 per license; fingerprints--$38.25.',
