    ('33', '01', 'SEP01', 'SEP',
     'Septic System Evaluator, Professional', '<p>A Professional Septic System Evaluator observes and determines the condition of a septic system at a point in time. They may perform professional services only when the evaluator is qualified by education and training in the specific technical areas involved.</p>
<p><strong>Legislation:</strong>&nbsp;NH RSA 310-A:202; Administrative Rules Chapter Sep 100 - 500</p>
<p><strong>Licensure Requirements:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Good professional character</li>
<li>Current Granite State Installers and Designers (GSDI) certified designers</li>
<li>Engaged in business for five years with a minimum of 25 evaluations</li>
<li>Certified designer who has performed a minimum of 25 evaluations</li>
<li>Submit application, with proof of passing exams, summary of technical experience, and three references</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Examination:</strong> Written and field examinations are required</p>
<p><strong>Continuing Education:</strong> Unknown at this time</p>
<p><strong>Fees:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Application: $100</li>
<li>Licensure: $50</li><
<li>Examination: unknown</li>
<li>Re-examination: unknown</li>
<li>Biennial renewal: $100</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Reciprocity:</strong> If, in the determination of the Board, another state grants reciprocity to residents of this state and is deemed to have state requirements equal to or exceeding to those of this state, the Board may enter into a reciprocity agreement allowing applicants who are certified under the laws of such other state to obtain a license as a septic system evaluator in this state upon such terms and conditions as may be determined by the Board</p>
<p>revised Jan 2018</p>',
     '0', '1', '1', '1', '2',
     '0', '0', '0', '9', '0',
     'https://www.nhes.nh.gov/elmi/products/licertocc/documents/septicevpr.pdf', '20240906'),
    ('33', '01', 'SEW01', 'SEW',
     'Individual Sewage Disposal System Designer/Installer', '<p>Individual sewage disposal system installers and designers design, install, and repair septic systems, which are required for any structure to which a water supply is connected and that is not connected to a municipal sewage collection system. Septic systems must be designed and installed by individuals holding a valid permit, issued by the New Hampshire Department of Environmental Services, Subsurface Systems Bureau. Additionally, repair or replacement of an existing septic system must be conducted by a permit holder as well. The only exception to this requirement is that a homeowner may repair or install a septic system for his/her primary domicile.</p>
<p><strong>Legislation:</strong>&nbsp;NH RSA 485-A:35-36; Administrative Rules Chapter Env-Wq 1000</p>
<p><strong>Permit Requirements:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Designers must pass a written examination and a field test for soil interpretation</li>
<li>Installers must pass a written examination</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Examination:</strong>&nbsp;Required. Installers must pass a two-part written exam, held twice each year. Designers must pass a three-part written exam, plus a field exam, held twice each year.</p>
<p><strong>Continuing Education:</strong>&nbsp;Three hours of DES-approved continuing education must be completed annually, for a total of six hours for renewal. Three of the six hours must be attained at educational seminars offered by Subsurface Systems Bureau staff, or pre-approved by the Bureau.</p>
<p><strong>Fees:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Examination: $80</li>
<li>Biennial renewal: $80</li>
</ul>
<ul>Licenses expire on December 31 of every other year</ul>
<p><strong>Reciprocity:</strong>&nbsp;Not permitted</p>
<p>revised Jan 2018</p>',
