    ('69', '01', '0000000007', '002',
     'Engineer, Professional', 'Description of Occupation:   Engineers design or develop solutions to problems or develop products or structures using the practical application of physical laws and engineering.  Engineering specialties include aeronauticle/aerospace, chemical, civil, computer, electrical, industrial, marine, structural, or mechanical.Requirements for Licensing:   Graduate of approved school,  four years full time lawful experience,  passed FE (EIT),  passed PE  
                                                                          or
Twelve years full time lawful experience,  passed FE (EIT),  passed PE.
For civil engineer must take and pass special seismic examination.Examinations:  See above.Application and Fees:  $500 application fee and $500 registration fee - after approval of application.Related Classification Codes:   DOT  002. 003  005  006  007  008  010  011  012  013  014  015    SOC- Division 16',
     '0', '3', '2', '9', '0',
     '2', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     NULL, '20100101'),
    ('69', '01', '0000000008', '002',
     'Land Surveyor', 'Description of Occupation:   A Land Surveyor plans, organizes and directs work of one or more survey parties engaged in surveying earth''s surface to determine precise location and measurements of points, elevations, lines, areas, and contours for construction, mapmaking, land division, titles, mining or other purposes.Requirements for Licensing:   Graduate of approved school,  four years full time lawful experience,  passed fundamentals LS exam, passed principles PLS exam,  passed CNMI land matters exam                      
                                                                                    or
                     Graduate of approved community school,  eight years full time lawful experience,  passed fundamentals LS exam,  passed principles PLS exam,  passed CNMI land matters exam                                                                   
                     
                                                                                     or
                      Twelve years full time lawful experience,  passed fundamentals LS exam,  passed principles PLS exam,  passed CNMI land matters exam.
                       
                      
Examinations:  See above.Application and Fees:   $500 application fee and $500 registration fee - after approval of application.Related Classification Codes:   DOT 018.167-018   SOC 1643',
     '0', '2', '2', '9', '0',
     '2', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     NULL, '20100101'),
    ('69', '01', '0000000009', '003',
     'Midwife', 'Description of Occupation:   A midwife provides prenatal care to pregnant women, delivers babies in the home or in the hospital, and provides postnatal care.Requirements for Licensing:  US Citizen, residence or under employment contract (1-2 years).  If foreign worker must sit for  national examination and hold active license in own country.  Proof of active practice in past nine months.  Certificate or diploma from program of midwifery.  Thirty hours of continueing education units for renewal.Examinations:  See Above.  Application and Fees:  $60.00Related Classification Codes:   DOT  075.264-014   SOC  29',
     '0', '2', '2', '1', '0',
     '9', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     NULL, '20100101'),
    ('69', '01', '0000000010', '003',
     'Registered Nurse/Advanced Practice Nurse', 'Description of Occupation:   A registered Nurse observes, assesses, and records symptoms, reactions, and progress of patients.  An RN administers medications, assists in convalescence and rehabilitation, instructs patients and their families in proper care, and helps individuals and groups to improve or maintain their health.Requirements for Licensing:   US Citizenship, residence or under employment contract (1-2 yrs).  Completion of ASN/BSN program.  If foriegn must sit for their national exam and hold active license in their country.  Proof of active nursing practice in past nine months.  Education - ASN/BSN or advanced practice.  Thirty hours continueing education units for renewal.Examinations:   National Council Licensure Exam.Application and Fees:   Application $10.  RN exam  $80,  RN endorsement  $60.Related Classification Codes:   DOT  075.374-010   SOC  29',
     '0', '2', '9', '9', '0',
     '0', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     NULL, '20100101'),
    ('69', '01', '0000000011', '003',
     'Nurse, Licensed Practical', 'Description of Occupation:   A Licensed Practical Nurse provides prescribed medical treatment and personal care services to ill, injured, convalescent, and handicapped persons in such settings as hospitals, clinics, private homes, schools, sanitariums, and similar institutions;   takes and records temperature, blood pressure, and pulse;  dresses wounds;  administers specific medication;  performs limited routine laboratory work;  prepares food trays and feeds patients;  bathes, dresses, and assists patients in walking and turning;  makes beds;  and may assist in delivery, care, and feediing of infants.Requirements for Licensing:   Citizenship, residence or under employment contract (1-2 yrs).  Completion of ASN/BSN program.  If licensed in US can transfer valid license to CNMI.Examinations:   National Council Licensure ExamApplication and Fees:   $60.00Related Classification Codes:   DOT  079.374-014   SOC  573',
     '0', '2', '2', '9', '0',
     '9', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     NULL, '20100101'),
    ('69', '01', '0000000012', '004',
     'Optometrist', 'Description of Occupation:   An Optometrist examines, diagnosis, and treats conditions of the vision system.  Prescribes corrective procedures;  conserves, improves, and corrects vision through the use of lenses, contact lenses, and prisms.Requirements for Licensing:  Any person, not heretofore licensed to practice optometry in the CNMI, who desires and intends to commence such practice must comply with the following requirements:  File proof of his qualifications, pay the prescribed fees.
Proof of qualifications:  Satisfactory evidence must be filed with the Board showing the following qualifications.  Age not less than 21 years,  Citizenship,  good moral character,  graduation from a US accredited school of optometry recognized by the Board,  maintaining a standard of six college years and including, as a prerequisite to admission to the courses in optometry, at least two academic years of study in a college of arts and sciences.Examinations:   Endorsement.  Must submit a status report of license from National Practioner Data Bank.  1-800-767-6732.Application and Fees:   $50.00Related Classification Codes:   DOT 079.101-018   SOC  281',
     '0', '9', '2', '9', '0',
     '9', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     NULL, '20100101'),
    ('69', '01', '0000000013', '004',
     'Pharmacist', 'Description of Occupation:   A pharmacist interprets prescriptions and compounds or dispenses medications ordered by physicians, dentists, or other authorized prescribers.  Maintains proper records of them;  stores and preserves biologicals, vaccines, and other drugs subject to deterioration, advises patients on the therapeutic values, contents, hazards, and uses of drugs and related devices;  provides drug information to other medical professionals;  and reviews drug utilization of patients.Requirements for Licensing:   An applicant to become a registered pharmacist in the CNMI must:  be of good moral character,  be a graduate of and have an academic degree from a college of pharmacy or department of pharmacy of a university accredited by the American Council on Pharmaceutical Education and/or approved by the Board;  have satisfactorily passed an examination approved by the Board;  is lawfully entitled to remain and work in the CNMI;  have completed one year of practical pharmaceutical experience or pharmacy internship.Examinations:   Endorsement.  Must submit status report of license from National Practitioner Data Bank.  1-800-767-6732.Application and Fees:  $50.00Related Classification Codes:   DOT  074.161-010   SOC 301',
     '0', '2', '2', '9', '0',
     '2', '1', '9', '9', '0',
     NULL, '20100101'),
    ('69', '01', '0000000014', '004',
     'Physician', 'Description of Occupation:   A Physician diagnoses and treats diseases and disorders of the human body;  examines patients, utilizing all types of medical equipment, instruments, and tests following standard medical procedures;  performs surgery;  prescribes and administers medications; and engages in research to aid in control and cure of disease.

Requirements for Licensing:   Every person desiring to practice medicine shall, before beginning to practice, procure from the Board a license or permit authorizing such practice.  A license or permit may be issued to any person who:  is lawfully entitled to remain and work in the CNMI;  and has completed one year of postgraduate training approved by the Board.
Minimum educational requirement:  graduation with degree of Doctor of Medicine from a US or Canadian medical school listed as approved by the Council on Medical Education and Hospitals, American Medical Association in the list published for the year of the applicant''s graduation;  or  graduation with degree of Doctor of Medicine or equivalent degree from a medical school other than one covered by the paragraph above (including foreign schools), provided that the medical education and the  medical knowledge acquired therefrom are substantially comparable to that of approved US medical schools as provided in the paragraph above, as determined by the Board.

Examinations:    Endorsement.  Must submit a status report of license from the National Practitioner Data Bank.  1-800-767-6732.

Application and Fees:    $100.00

Related Classification Codes:   DOT 070.101-022   SOC 61',
     '0', '2', '2', '9', '0',
     '0', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     NULL, '20100101'),
    ('69', '01', '0000000015', '004',
     'Physician''s Assistant/Medex', 'Description of Occupation:   A Physician Assistant is a skilled health practitioner qualified through academic and clinical training to serve patients.  Under the supervision and responsibility of a licensed physician, the physician assistant performs medical tasks normally carried out by a physician, such as taking medical histories, performing physical examinations, ordering laboratory tests, making tentative diagnoses, and prescribing appropriate treatments.  The physician assistant always works under the direction of a licensed "supervising physician".Requirements for Licensing:   An applicant for certification as a physician''s assistant must:  be graduated from high school, or possess an equivalent educational background;  be able to communicate adequately in the English language;  be of good moral character and reputation;  and have attended and completed a course in training as a physician''s assistant approved by the Council on Medical Education of the American Medical Association, or other course approved by the Board.Examinations:  Endorsement.  Must submit a status report of license from the National Practitioner Data Bank.  1-800-767-6732.Application and Fees:  $50.00Related Classification Codes:  DOT 079.364-018   SOC 304',
     '0', '0', '1', '9', '0',
     '0', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     NULL, '20100101'),
    ('69', '01', '0000000016', '004',
     'Clinical Psychologist', 'Description of Occupation:   A Clinical Psychologist is a sub-specialty in psychology which is primarily concerned with assessing and alleviating emotional, mental, and behavioral disorders in a hospital, institution, or other clinical setting.Requirements for Licensing:   "Clinical Psychologist" means a person who has received training in clinical psychology from an accredited school in the US and has completed the internship requirements.  Training means doctoral level training in clinical psychology at an accredited institution of higher learning in the US.  Accredited means that the college or the university has met the standards as established either by the Middle States Association of Colleges and Secondary Schools, or by the New England Association of Colleges and Secondary Schools, or by the North Central Association of Schools and Colleges, or by other accrediting agencies using similar standards.  Internship means a training program of one year that is supervised by a doctoral level clinical psychologist or approved by the American Psychological Association, and can be demonstrated to be of high quality.Examinations:   Endorsement.  Must submit a status report of license from National Practitioner Data Bank.  1-800-767-6732.Application and Fees:   $50.00Related Classification Codes:    DOT  045.107-022   045.061-018   SOC  1915',
     '0', '2', '2', '9', '0',
     '2', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     NULL, '20100101'),
    ('69', '01', '0000000017', '004',
     'Radiologic Technologist/Technician', 'Description of Occupation:   A Radiologic Technologist operates radiologic equipment to produce radiographs (x rays) of body for diagnostic purposes, as directed by Radiologist.  Positions patient on examining table and adjusts immobilization devices to obtain optimum views of specified area of body requested by physician.  Moves x-ray equipment into specified position and adjusts equipment controls to set exposure factors.Requirements for Licensing:   A license may be issued to any person who: 1. Has completed a two year training course in diagnostic radiologic technology or radiotherapeutic technology which has been approved by the Board, The American Register of Radiologic Technologist, or the American Medical Association,  2. Shows evidence acceptable to the Board of possessing knowledge of such subjects as:  radiation protection standards and practices;  basic human anatomy and physiology;  basic physics including concepts of energy, electric power and circuits,  and the properties of X-rays;  radiographic exposure techniques; and histology. 3. Has one year of experience in one or more combination of types of experience such as:  training and experience as a radiologic technologist, nuclear medicine technician, diagnostic ultrasound technician, radiologic technician.  Training  and experience as a practical nurse, nurses aid, nurses assistant,student nurse, or registered nurse.  A medical aid, medical technician, laboratory technician, laboratory assistant, or similar type of position in a medical, clinical, college or industrial laboratory.  A radiology technician in a non-medical capacity.  4.  Shows service; education and training in the armed forces medical corps as an X-ray technician.Examinations:   Endorsement.  Must submit status report of license from National Practitioner Data Bank.  1-800-767-6732.Application and Fees:   $50.00Related Classification Codes:   DOT  078.362-026   SOC  365',
     '0', '9', '2', '9', '0',
     '2', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     NULL, '20100101'),
    ('69', '01', '0000000018', '002',
     'Real Estate Appraiser, Federally Related Transactions', 'Description of Occupation :   A Real Estate Appraiser, Federal Transactions  appraises real property to determine fair value and assesses taxes in accordance with prescribed schedules:  Inspects property and considers factors such as currrent market value,  location of property,  and building or replacement costs to make property appraisal.  Computes amount of tax to be levied, using applicable tax tables, and writes reports of determinations for public record.Requirements for licensing:	Licensed Appraiser:  75 classroom hours of courses in subjects related to Real Estate Appraiser including coverage of USPAP.
	Successful completion of the Uniform State Certification/Licensing Examination or its equivalent,  2000 hours of experience.
	
	
	Certified Residential Appraiser:  105 classroom hours of courses in subjects related to real estate appraiser including coverage of USPAP  (105 may include the 75 classroom hours of the licensed classification requirement above).  Successful completion of the Uniform State Certification/Licensing Examination or its equivalent,  2000 hours of experience (at least 50% of the experience must have been in major residential appraisal work).	Certified General Appraiser:  165 classroom hours of courses in subjects related to Real Estate Appraiser including coverage of USPAP,  (165 may include the 75 and 105 classroom hours required on the above classifications),  Succesful completion of the Uniform State Certification/Licensing Examination or its equivalent,  2000 hours of experience (at least 50% of the experience must have been in non-residential appraisal work).See above.     Application fee		                    $ 100.00
     Certification fee		                       100.00
     Annual fee (Federal Registry)		     25.00
     Local Exam		                                75.00	   
     RenewaL fee (Biannual)                       50.00                         Related classification codes:   DOT  188.167-010   SOC  1135',
     '0', '2', '1', '9', '0',
     '2', '0', '9', '9', '0',
     NULL, '20100101'),
    ('69', '01', '0000000019', '002',
     'Real Estate Appraiser, Non Federal', 'Description of Occupation:  A Real Estate Appraiser, Non -  Federal,  appraises improved or unimproved real property to determine value for purchase, sale, investment, mortgage, or loan purposes. Interviews persons familiar with property and immediate surroundings, such as contractors, home owners, and other realtors to obtain pertinent information.  Inspects property for construction, condition, and functional design and takes property measurements.  Considers factors, such as depreciation, reproduction costs, value comparison of similar property, and income potential, when computing final estimation of property value.  Searches public records for transactions, such as sales, leases, and assessments.  Prepares written report, utilizing data collected and submits report to corroborate value established.Requirements for Licensing:  
					
								Bachelors Degree or Higher
								w/15 Classroom hours appraisal
								related courses
								Two Years Experience
								Local Examination
								              or
								 AA Degree in Business
								 30 Credit Hours of Appraisal Related Courses
								 Four Years Experience
								 Local Examination
								 
								              or
								 50 Credit Hours of Appraisal Related Courses
								 Six Years Experience             
								 Local Examination
Licensed Residential - At least 50% of the experience must have been in major residential appraisal work.
Licensed General - At least 50% of the experience must have been in major non-residential appraisal work.
Applicants for the Non-Federal related transactions must meet the education, experience and must take and pass the local appraisal examination approved by the Board, possess a reputation for honesty, truthfulness, fairness and financial integrity and has not been convicted of a crime related to the real estate appraisal profession.
All applicants must submit three notarized references from individuals who have had dealings with the applicant relating to the applicant''s real estate appraisal experience and to verify the reputation for honesty, truthfulness, fairness and financial integrity of the applicant.
All licenses/certificates expire two years following issuance or renewal and become invalid after that date unless renewed.  You are required to keep this office informed of any change in your mailing address.Examinations:   Local appraisal exam.Application and Fees:                                  Schedule of Fees
Application Fee	                                              	  $100.00		
License of Certificate Fee				          $100.00
Renewal Fee							              50.00
Inactive Fee							                     20.00
Reactivation Fee						              20.00
Restoration Fee						                     20.00
Recognition of License/Certificate (Temp.)              25.00        
Local Examination Fee                                             75.00                                        Examination fee shall be as provided by contract with a professional testing organization.Related Classification Codes:   DOT 191.267-010    SOC  4123',
     '0', '1', '2', '9', '0',
     '2', '1', '9', '9', '0',
     NULL, '20100101'),
    ('69', '01', '0000000020', '002',
     'Harbor Pilot', 'Description of Occupation:   A Harbor Pilot commands ships to steer them into and out of harbors, estuaries, straits, and sounds.  Directs course and speed of ship on basis of specialized knowledge of local winds, weather, tides, and current.  Orders worker at helm to steer ship, and navigates ship to avoid reefs, outlying shoals, and other hazards to shipping, utilizing aids to navigation, such as lighthouses and bouys.  Signals tugboat captain to berth and unberth ship.Requirements for Licensing:   Meets age requirement  (21- 72), US citizen or CNMI Resident,  Physically Capable (As certified by a CNMI Licensed Physician)  Mentally Fit and Competent (As certified by a CNMI Licensed Physician),  20/20 corrected vision and normal color vision (As certified by a CNMI Licensed Optometrist),  Speak, write and comprehend english language.
Has successfully completed all examinations required by the board that are applicable to the pilotage district for which the applicant seeks licensure.
Completed/Documented Familiarization Trips.
 
 													Satisfies at least one of the following:
					2.1.  Holds a valid U.S.C.G. First class Pilot License for vessels of unlimited tonnage and endorsed for one, or more of the pilotage districts of the CNMI and must document, to the satisfaction of the Board, at least 30 safe vessel movements, within the preceeding year (25% of movements must be at night), in any ports of the United States, or former Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands, as a harbor pilot working under the authority of his USCG First Class Pilot License; or
					
					2.2  Possesses a U.S. Coast Guard Master or Mate''s license of unlimited tonnage on steam or motor vessels upon oceans ( (excluding fishing vessels);  or 
					 
					 2.3.  Possesses a U.S. Coast Guard Master or Mate''s license of not less than 1600 gross tons on steam or motor vessels upon oceans ( excluding fishing vessels );  or
					
					 2.4.  Possesses a valid U.S. Coast Guard License with a rating as Master on Steam or Motor Vessels of 500 gross tons including freight or towing vessels ( excluding fishing vessels );  and , two-thirds of the required number of vessel movements for that pilotage district have been on vessels of 500 gross tons or more; or
					
					2.5.  Previously held a Trust Territory deck officers license for vessels over 500 gross tons and can demonstrate to the satisfaction of the Board one year sea time as Master or Mate of steam or motor vessels of 500 gross tons or more;  or
					
					2.6.  Previously held a Trust Territory Harbor Pilot''s license for vessels of at least 500 gross tons for the ports in the CNMI and can document to the satisfaction of the Board, at least an average of 10 vessel movements per year in the immediately preceeding 5 years in the applicable pilotage district.
 													
 					 
 					The harbor pilot license shall be limited to the tonnage licensed by the U.S.C.G. or as previously licensed by the Trust Territory government.
 No person shall be licensed by the Board unless he or she has applied to the Board for a pilot license on the form provided by the Board and paid to the CNMI Treasurer the application fee of $100.00.  All application fees are nonrefundable.
There are further provisions for familiarization trips;  Physical requirements;  Radar training; and drug screening set forth in the Commonwealth Register,  Volume 18 number 11 page 14662. 							Examinations:   Each applicant shall be required to pass examinations:
   
 	(a)  Applicants applying under the provisions of Part 2,   (2.2), (2.3), and (2.4) shall take and pass the U.S.C.G. written examinations covering the international rules of the road, and the physical characteristics of the pilotage district for which the applicant seeks licensure.
       (b)  Applicants applying under the provisions of Part 2, (2.5) and (2.6) shall obtain a U.S. Coast Guard first class
       pilots licensure.							
	(c)  Applicants applying with Coast Guard licenses under the provisions of Part 2.    		(2.1) are exempt from the examination requirements.Application and Fees:   $100    License fee $500,  renewal fee at two years $500.
                                           
Related Classification Codes:   DOT  197.133-026    SOC  8241',
